{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AMMe1VFQhKGGXvA5iTqAwg","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (120.3KB). Full output saved to: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-162042--x14746a-work/b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240/tool-results/bgv49cumv.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\ntotal 136\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha   4096 Nov  5 16:20 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha   4096 Nov  5 16:20 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 110276 Nov  5 16:20 PLAN.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha    219 Nov  5 16:20 STAGE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha  12611 Nov  5 16:20 TASK.md\n----\n# Game plan: a gothic action RPG for the TV, inspired by Diablo 3\n\n**Genre:** co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash loot game). **Camera:** fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic.\n**Players:** 1 to 4, on one screen or in AI Hub sessions. **Engine:** the Pixel Engine kit (0.46.0 when this was written).\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- TASK.md is the CEO's brief and wins over this plan. This plan decides everything TASK.md leaves open, so you can build without asking.\n- Read in this order: this plan, then the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md` whole, then the hub's `README.md` whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- **Build order is in section 15.** Follow it. The deliverable HTML must build and run from the first milestone on, and every milestone leaves the game playable.\n- Lines marked **(verified)** were run on this computer against engine 0.46.0 in a throwaway build. Trust them. If your kit is newer, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first.\n- Numbers are starting values. Anything about balance lives in one table (`src/data/tuning.js`) and shows up as a dev-mode slider.\n- No more source research is needed. Section 1 says what to take from the source.\n- All paths are relative to your dev folder. Your TASK.md gives the names and places of the dev folder, the deliverable and the exit interview file.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240","uuid":"2507026f-6f48-4e84-9a9e-73b7e02d28bf","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:45.852Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 136\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha   4096 Nov  5 16:20 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha   4096 Nov  5 16:20 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 110276 Nov  5 16:20 PLAN.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha    219 Nov  5 16:20 STAGE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha  12611 Nov  5 16:20 TASK.md\n----\n# Game plan: a gothic action RPG for the TV, inspired by Diablo 3\n\n**Genre:** co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash loot game). **Camera:** fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic.\n**Players:** 1 to 4, on one screen or in AI Hub sessions. **Engine:** the Pixel Engine kit (0.46.0 when this was written).\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- TASK.md is the CEO's brief and wins over this plan. This plan decides everything TASK.md leaves open, so you can build without asking.\n- Read in this order: this plan, then the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md` whole, then the hub's `README.md` whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- **Build order is in section 15.** Follow it. The deliverable HTML must build and run from the first milestone on, and every milestone leaves the game playable.\n- Lines marked **(verified)** were run on this computer against engine 0.46.0 in a throwaway build. Trust them. If your kit is newer, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first.\n- Numbers are starting values. Anything about balance lives in one table (`src/data/tuning.js`) and shows up as a dev-mode slider.\n- No more source research is needed. Section 1 says what to take from the source.\n- All paths are relative to your dev folder. Your TASK.md gives the names and places of the dev folder, the deliverable and the exit interview file.\n- **Other agents share this computer.** TASK.md's \"This computer\" notes apply as written, with one correction (section 2, item 5). Stay out of the folders beside yours. Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp`, which is shared. Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`; the browsers are already downloaded. Keep one or two browsers open at most. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n- **No stubs.** If you run out of budget, cut whole features in the order given in section 15, never halfway. A feature that isn't there is fine; a menu item that does nothing is not.\n\n### What you hand back\n\n1. **One HTML file,** named by the pattern in your TASK.md and placed where it says, at the root above the dev folder. It holds the whole game: the engine, your code and the hub client, inlined. TASK.md allows CDN dependencies, but this game needs none.\n2. **The exit interview file** beside that HTML, in the exact shape in section 17.\n3. **Your final chat reply,** carrying Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback. The same four appear on the in-game About/Credits screen (section 13).\n\n**The bar:** a feature-complete demo, treated as a complete, polished vertical slice, long enough to show every feature and mechanic so a player understands how the game plays and feels and what they can and can't do. It carries all the infrastructure the rest of the game would need. No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features.\n\n### The title\n\n**Pick it first:** the names of your dev folder and your HTML include it. As TASK.md says: a playful riff on the source's name; silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator by running code. Make it a pun that reads as its own name, not the source's name with a letter changed. It lives in one constant, `TITLE` in `src/data/meta.js`, and appears only in the game's frame: title screen, attract overlay, session card, About, the no-WebGPU page, `<title>`, the engine's game `name` and the save key. Story text never mentions it, so the world below works with any title.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n### Pitch\n\nForty days ago the great bell that kept the dead asleep cracked. It still tolls every dusk, but now it says *get up*, and everything buried does. You got up too, except that you kept your wits. Pick a trade (swing a holy bell on a chain, hurl lanterns, or bury your problems with a shovel), smash hordes that burst apart, chase loot that rings like church bells, and climb the broken tower to silence the walled-up saint who has been screaming at you the whole way. Alone, on a couch, or online.\n\n### Pillars (when in doubt, these decide)\n\n1. **Every button press breaks something.** Hits have weight: flash, shake, knockback, ragdolls, shattered props. Hordes are the point.\n2. **Loot you can hear.** Rarity is a colour, a beam and a sound. Legendary drops ring a bell. Good items change how skills work, not only the numbers.\n3. **Builds are play styles.** Six slots, eight skills per class, three variations of each. Swapping is free and instant, so experimenting costs nothing.\n4. **Gothic melodrama with a straight face and a black joke.** Villains proclaim. Townsfolk shrug. Nobody winks at the camera.\n5. **A TV game.** Big, readable, one screen at a time, playable from a sofa with a pad. Mouse and keyboard feel native on the same screens.\n6. **One more run.** After the story, a repeatable run mode with a meter, a boss and rising difficulty.\n\n### What we take from the source, and what we make ours\n\nTASK.md asks you to capture what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, and turn it into mechanics, pacing and UX. So the first rows are the feel of the thing, made ours.\n\n| What the source does well | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| A gothic land where hell climbs up through its graveyards, and a doomed town holding out | **Vespermoor** and **Tollhaven** |\n| A hero who is more than mortal | **The Late**: dead, up again, and still themselves |\n| A grim old keeper of lore who reads you the history | **Crier Maudlin**, a severed head who never stopped working |\n| Themes of apocalypse, failing faith and old sins coming due | A cracked holy bell, a wronged saint, a bishop in debt |\n| Hordes that explode, physical destruction | Ragdolls, shatter props, a kill-streak bonus called **Requiem** |\n| Classes with their own resource | **Bellwarden** (Clangor), **Lamplighter** (Wick), **Sexton** (Tithe) |\n| Skills with swappable modifiers | 8 skills per class, each with 3 **Tones** |\n| Passive choices | 4 **Vows** per class, pick 2 |\n| Life pickups that drop in combat | **Tallow**: wax drops that re-melt your life candle |\n| Beams and sounds for great loot | Coloured beams, and a bell strike for every legendary |\n| Elites with random powers | Elite packs with **Afflictions** |\n| The loot creature you chase | **The Collection Plate**: a living offering dish on little legs, spilling coins as it runs |\n| Risky chests | **Unquiet Coffins**: open one, survive 45 seconds, get paid |\n| Temporary shrines | **Votive Shrines** |\n| Repeatable random dungeon with a meter and a boss | **Knell Runs**; the timed version is the **Grand Knell** |\n| Endless levelling | **Devotion** |\n| Difficulty ladder | **Pilgrim, Zealot, Martyr, Penance I to V** |\n| Crafting artisans | **Dame Brasse** the bellfounder, **Mother Ogive** at the Reliquary |\n| Extracting item powers | The **Reliquary** holds three extracted powers |\n| Waypoints, town portal | **Lychgates**, the **Passing Bell** |\n| An AI companion | **Sister Pyx** |\n| Couch and online co-op | Couch co-op on its own, shared-world sessions in AI Hub |\n| Scheming lords of the pit who taunt you while you play | **The Bursar** and **Malison**, in subtitled gibberish |\n\n### Stay original\n\nThis is a new game in the same genre, with its own world, cast, art, music and levels. TASK.md says so and it's a hard rule.\n\n- Never use the source's names or look: Sanctuary, Tristram, Caldeum, Westmarch, Horadrim, Nephalem, Cain, Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Imperius, Malthael, Azmodan, Belial, Kanai, Paragon, Torment, its class names and resource names, its famous lines, its melodies, or a HUD built around two orbs. No fallen star, no amnesiac angel.\n- The source is credited by name only on the About screen and in the exit interview.\n- `tools/test.mjs` greps `src/` for these names as whole words (section 16).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Facts verified on this computer\n\nThese save you hours. All were run in a real build here.\n\n**Bundling**\n1. **(verified)** One HTML file boots on `file://` with WebGPU. Bundle with `esbuild game.js --bundle --format=esm --target=es2022 --charset=utf8 --legal-comments=none`, put the result in one inline `<script type=\"module\">`. The engine has no `import.meta` and needs no fetch, so its built-in models and Rapier's wasm work from the file. The result is about 5.3 MB plus your code, with nothing external. Don't minify your own code: the agent that imports the game adds its usage figures to the About screen later, so keep strings findable.\n2. Escape `</script` as `<\\/script` in the bundle. If you insert with `String.replace`, use a function replacer, or `$&` in the code corrupts the file.\n3. **(verified)** Page order that the hub accepts: `<meta charset>`, then `session.js` byte for byte as the first script, then the `hub:*` metas, then `<title>`. Body: `<div id=\"app\"></div>` and the module script.\n4. **(verified)** If you ever need a pack, set `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__['packs/x.json'] = 'data:application/json;base64,...'` in a classic script before the module, then `loadPack('packs/x.json')` works on `file://`. The built-in library is enough for this game, so prefer not to.\n\n**Browsers in tests**\n5. **(verified) Use the kit's flags:** `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` (the list in `check.mjs`). The shorter list in the \"This computer\" notes lost the GPU device within seconds on this engine (`A valid external Instance reference no longer exists`). That error is the flags, not your game.\n6. **(verified)** Software rendering is slow: about 20 fps for a small scene at 1280×720, 3 to 8 fps at 1080p with 40 rigged monsters. So tests advance time with `engine.step(n)` (120 frames took 6 ms in a small scene) and read pixels with `await engine.renderer.capture()` (about 180 ms). Never wait in real time when you can step.\n7. **(verified)** `Engine.headless` works in Node with `buildModel`, `compileClips`, mixers, HUD calls and audio counts. `hud.width` is 480 and `hud.canvas` is null there. `node check.mjs --headless game.js --keys KeyD*30` plays and replays to the same fingerprint. Replays still match with ragdolls and flying chunks in play. Every module must guard `window`, `document` and `navigator` with `typeof`.\n\n**The hub**\n8. **(verified)** A full engine in the harness's iframes passes: 2 players; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; the same with `scenario=host-leave`; 2 players with `scenario=server`; and a game-owned card. Copies were ready in 2 to 6.5 seconds.\n9. **(verified)** For `AIHub.server` (a copy with no player) use `Engine.headless(game, {})` inside the iframe and drive it with a timer: ready in 0.6 s, no GPU, harness passes. Step by elapsed time, not a fixed count.\n10. **(verified)** On the game's own card, answer Enter from a plain `window` `keydown` listener, not `wasPressed`. The harness allows 3 seconds and software-rendered frames are slow.\n11. The hub's clocks (from `round.js`): hard limit is `hub:session-minutes` × 1.5, at most 15 minutes; 5 s grace after `time-up`; 3 s countdown; 20 s load grace; 20 s hidden-host limit.\n\n**Characters and animation**\n12. **(verified)** You can build any humanoid as a `ModelDef` of nested pivots named after `HERO_RIG`'s joints, and `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, build.object)` animates it with all 78 hero clips, including `Slash1`, `Slash2`, `Slash3`, `Slam`, `LeapSlam`, `Spin`, `Cast`, `CastBig`, `CastWeapon`, `CastBigWeapon`, `BowDraw`, `BowRelease`, `Roll`, `Charge`, `Shout`, `HitReact`, `Death`, `Triumph`. `new Ragdoll(physics, root, HERO_RAGDOLL)` works on the same body. Template in section 8.\n13. **(verified)** All 15 joints must exist or `compileClips` throws `rig joint \"ForearmR\" not found`. An empty pivot is fine, which keeps cheap monsters cheap (14 meshes against 21).\n14. **(verified)** Compile clips once per body type (63 ms for 78 clips) and share them: `new THREE.AnimationMixer(instance).clipAction(clip)` moves a second instance. `buildModel` is cheap and shares geometry.\n15. **(verified)** Custom clips: `{ name, frames, fast, keys: [[frame, { ArmR: [x, y, z], Pelvis: { p: [x, y, z] } }, 'outCubic'], ...] }` and `gaitClip(HERO_RIG, {...})` compile with `validateClip` clean.\n16. **(verified)** `analyzeClip` throws on merged pivot bodies because its sole meshes are gone. Run it on the engine's own hero inside `Engine.headless`: `const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL)`, same rig, same results. It finds real problems such as `planted foot slides 0.80 m/s`; tune `speed` and `stance` until it's clean.\n17. **(verified)** To attack while running, filter a compiled clip to upper-body tracks: `new THREE.AnimationClip(name, clip.duration, clip.tracks.filter(t => UPPER.has(t.name.split('.')[0])))` and play it over `Run`. Track names look like `Torso.quaternion`. It ran without error; check how it looks.\n\n**Pause, input, camera, HUD**\n18. **(verified) Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`.** `update` keeps running with `dt` 0, `fixedUpdate` stops, `wasPressed` still works, so menus work. `engine.paused = true` stops `update` too: never use it for menus. Menu animation counts rendered frames, since `dt` is 0.\n19. `hitstop` and `timeScale` stall the whole simulation. Never use them on a session host. Use them solo and on the couch only.\n20. **(verified)** Use `aspect: 'fill'`. The default letterboxes (at 1280×720 you get 960×540 in the middle). With `fill` the art size changes with the window: 427×240 at 720p, 480×270 at 1080p and 4K, 512×288 at 1440p, about 136×276 on a portrait phone. Lay out from `hud.width` and `hud.height`, never from 480×270.\n21. **(verified)** The `iso` camera with yaw 45 looks toward −X −Z: screen right is (+0.71, 0, −0.71), screen up is (−0.71, 0, −0.71). So a wall on a room's −X or −Z side shows its face to the camera, and a wall on the +X or +Z side blocks the view.\n22. **(verified)** `camera: { preset: 'iso', viewHeight: 11.5, pitch: 42, yaw: 45 }` looks right at 1080p: a hero about 25 art pixels tall, with 40 monsters on screen and still readable. A `viewHeight` of 13 still reads, which gives the room to zoom out for co-op.\n23. **(verified)** The rig zooms on the wheel after `update` runs. Read `ctx.input.wheel` yourself, then set `ctx.input.wheel = 0` to stop it. `setZoom` clamps to `minZoom` and `maxZoom`.\n24. **(verified)** `hud.text` uppercases for you and draws `?` for any character it lacks. It has digits, A to Z, straight and curly quotes, the backtick and `. , : ; - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ ! ? < > # * @ ° · & | ~ ^ $`. No em dash, no ×, no arrows. Text is 6 art pixels per character (minus one) and 7 tall. `hudSprite` icons are about 12×12. A test lints all strings against this set.\n25. **(verified)** The engine's `Input` merges every pad into one, which can't do couch co-op. Set `ctx.input.gamepadButtons = {}` and read `navigator.getGamepads()` yourself, per pad (guard with `typeof`). A stub via `addInitScript` reaches both. The kit's pad mapping is for the platformer and would press Space, KeyJ and so on.\n26. Mouse buttons are keys (`MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`), and the canvas already blocks the context menu. The HUD canvas has `pointer-events: none`, so the pointer reaches the game through it.\n\n**Audio**\n27. **(verified)** Engine defaults are master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, saved under `pixel-engine:audio`, a key shared by every engine game on the same origin. So on first run (your own flag in storage) call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. `audio.resetVolumes()`, which the engine's settings panel calls, puts it back to 0.6, so wrap that method on the instance to re-apply 0.1. That doesn't touch `pixel-engine.js`.\n\n**Touch and look**\n28. **(verified)** Detect touch-only with `!matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` (true on Chromium's Pixel 7 profile, false on desktop). `touch: false` removes the joystick. The ⚙ button stays visible. WebKit here has no WebGPU, so test touch in Chromium.\n29. **(verified) The mood:** `engine.sun.color.setHex(PALETTE.sky); engine.sun.intensity = 0.55; engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy); engine.ambient.intensity = 0.9; scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.ink);` plus library torches and a warm light following the hero gives a rich dark dungeon. A tall thin `glowMaterial` box plus a coloured point light on the floor makes a superb loot beam.\n30. **(verified)** Floor textures at full white are too loud against monsters. `toonMaterial(PALETTE.mist, { map })` pushes them back and monsters read much better; `flagstones` tinted that way looked best, and `ashlar` is busy, so keep it for walls. Bright red `Decals` at 1 to 2 m look cartoonish; `plum` at 0.4 to 0.9 m sits right.\n\n**Randomness**\n31. **(verified) `Rng.fork` depends on the parent's current state,** whatever the docs' comment suggests: once the parent has drawn a number, the same `fork('zone:1')` gives different numbers. So keep one root `new Rng(seed)` per world that only forks and never draws. Forks from that root are stable, whatever their children draw. Every session client relies on this to build the same map.\n\n**Also run under `Engine.headless` and working (verified):** `Breakables` (`add`, then `break`); `Timeline` (cues, `line()`, `bars()`, `skip()`); `NavGrid` (`flowTo`, `flowDirection`, `path`, `lineOfSight`, `setOpen`); `LegStepper` with `twoBoneIK`; `SpringChain`; `Squash`; `Decals`; `Trail`; `Precipitation`; `WaterSurface`; `groundFog`; `{ dissolve: true }` clones; `glowMaterial` swaps; `mergeStaticMeshes` with a tinted `textureMap`; `disposeObject` on a zone root; `camera.rayAt` onto the ground; `lights.request` with `lifetime` and `priority`; `screen.cover`, `reveal`, `shockwave` and `flash`; `input.send` reaching `input.commands`; `audio.loop` with `spatialize`; the bell as sine `layers` (peak 0.84 from `renderSound`); `parseSong` on the Clemency motif. These ran without error; how they look is yours to check.\n\n**This engine start worked (verified):**\n\n```js\nconst engine = await Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({\n  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n  camera: { preset: 'iso', viewHeight: 11.5, pitch: 42, yaw: 45, minZoom: 0.6, maxZoom: 1.4 },\n  aspect: 'fill', quality: 'high', touch: false,\n}));\n```\n\nAdd `settings: { button: touchOnly ? 'top-right' : false }` to that **(verified: `false` removes the button)**. Your own Settings screen replaces the ⚙ everywhere except touch-only devices, where TASK.md asks for it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. World, story and tone\n\n### The world\n\n**Vespermoor** is a drowned moorland kingdom under one enormous tower, the **Belfry of Saint Malison**. In it hangs the Great Bell, **Old Clemency**. For a thousand years it tolled at dusk, and the toll told everything below: *stay down*. Forty days ago it cracked. It still tolls. It now says something else.\n\n- **The Risen:** the mindless dead, up and about.\n- **The Unrung:** demons from Below, things no bell was ever rung for. They hate sound, and they've come up through the graves the dead left open.\n- **The Late (formally the Tollborn):** you. You died during the cracked toll, or just after, and got up with your mind intact. Townsfolk find this awkward. They call you *the Late* and ask you to wipe your feet. Death doesn't stick to you, which is why you respawn.\n- **Tollhaven:** the last parish with its lamps lit.\n\n**The truth:** Saint Malison, revered as the foundress who \"gave herself to the bell\", was walled up alive in the tower's foundation by the first bishops, singing. She has heard that bell every dusk for a thousand years. Bishop Ambrose, drowning in debts to a demon called the Bursar, cracked it for her. She wants one thing: to ring the Last Toll, **Eventide**, and wake everything ever buried. The only thing she still fears is the sound of Old Clemency rung true.\n\n### Cast\n\n| Who | Role | Voice and manner |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Crier Maudlin** | Quest giver, reads lore aloud. A severed head in a lantern on a post. Lost the body, kept the job. | High, reedy, unstoppable |\n| **Dame Brasse** | Bellfounder: salvage, craft, reforge. Melts church bells into swords. Half deaf. | LOUD. Everything shouted |\n| **Mother Ogive** | Keeper of the Reliquary: extract powers, sockets. Blind; knows relics by taste. | Soft, delighted, unsettling |\n| **Pennywhistle** | Merchant in a plague-doctor beak. | Cheerful, nasal, modest markups considering the apocalypse |\n| **Sister Pyx** | Companion. Armoured nun with a crossbow. | Deadpan |\n| **Bishop Ambrose Thrice-Buried** | Kindly, pale, keeps a spade in the coffin. The traitor. | Warm, then not |\n| **The Bursar** | Mid-game boss. A bloated demon accountant in a stolen cope. | Booming, delighted by interest |\n| **Malison, the Unrung Saint** | Final boss. Huge entombed saint wrapped in bell ropes. | Vast, layered, furious |\n| **The Narrator** | Opening and ending. | Deep and slow |\n\n### Tone\n\nThree registers, and write everything in one of them:\n\n1. **High gothic proclamation:** narration, lore books, Malison. Long vowels, doom, an ominous last line.\n2. **Gallows humour:** townsfolk, item flavour, death messages. Dry, never modern. No slang, no winks.\n3. **Boss taunts:** direct address, mid-fight, short.\n\nSubtitles fit two rows of about 60 characters, which every line below does. Lore books get a paged screen instead. Punctuation stays inside the HUD font (section 2, item 24). Use these as written and write the rest to match.\n\n**Narrator:** \"For a thousand years Old Clemency tolled at dusk, and the dead of Vespermoor stayed down.\" / \"Forty days ago the Great Bell cracked. It still tolls. It now says something else.\" / \"You were buried on the thirty-ninth day. It did not take.\"\n\n**Crier Maudlin:** \"Hear ye, hear ye! The dead are up, the roads are closed, and the fish is off. Also you. You are dead. Welcome back.\" / \"I lost the body at harvest and kept the job. No one told me to stop.\" / \"The parish asks that you wipe your feet. You have been in the ground.\"\n\n**Dame Brasse:** \"WHAT? SPEAK UP! I MELT BELLS FOR A LIVING!\" / \"THAT SWORD WAS A CHURCH BELL ON TUESDAY. HIT SOMETHING WITH IT AND IT STILL SAYS AMEN.\"\n\n**Mother Ogive:** \"Bring me the holy bones, child. I will know them by the taste.\" / \"A saint's knuckle. Very old. A little salty. She died angry.\"\n\n**Pennywhistle:** \"Everything is half price, dear friend. The other half is your soul, but we can discuss terms.\" / \"No refunds. No returns. No survivors, so far.\"\n\n**Sister Pyx:** \"I have killed four hundred things today and confessed to none of them.\" / \"Bless you. Duck.\" / \"You smell like a grave. It is an improvement on the last one.\"\n\n**Bishop Ambrose:** \"They have buried me three times, my child. One learns to keep a spade in the coffin.\"\n\n**The Bursar:** \"YOUR SOUL IS OVERDUE!\" / \"INTEREST COMPOUNDS, LITTLE CORPSE!\" / \"I HAVE YOU DOWN FOR ONE DEATH. I SEE IT WAS NOT COLLECTED.\" / \"FORECLOSURE!\"\n\n**Malison:** \"A THOUSAND YEARS I HAVE BEEN YOUR FOUNDATION STONE. NOW I SHALL BE YOUR CEILING.\" / \"THEY WALLED ME UP SINGING. DO YOU KNOW WHAT I LEARNED IN THE DARK? EVERY VERSE.\" / \"RUN, LITTLE RINGER. THE HOUR IS UNRUNG, AND IT IS YOURS.\" / \"LISTEN. THAT IS EVERY GRAVE YOU EVER WALKED ON, OPENING.\" / (on a hero's death) \"DOWN YOU GO. GIVE MY REGARDS TO THE WORMS.\"\n\n**Lore book:** \"THE THIRD NIGHT. THE GRAVES IN THE NEW YARD ARE EMPTY. THE GRAVES IN THE OLD YARD ARE EMPTY. I HAVE BEGUN DIGGING ONE FOR MYSELF, FOR THE COMPANY.\"\n\n**System lines:** Death: \"YOU HAVE DIED. AGAIN. IT IS BECOMING A HABIT.\" Level up: \"YOU FEEL MORE ALIVE. THIS IS NOT MEDICALLY POSSIBLE.\" Streak: \"37 KILL REQUIEM\".\n\n**Item flavour:** \"She rang it only once. It was enough.\" / \"The best one is still in him.\" / \"Smells of smoke and regret.\"\n\n### The story, in five quests\n\n| # | Quest | Where | What happens | Unlocks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Wipe Your Feet** | St. Clement's Yard | Your coffin bursts. Tutorial fights. The Crier's voice carries over the wall. Reach Tollhaven. | Town, Pennywhistle, potion |\n| 2 | **The Weeping Causeway** | The Causeway | Find Dame Brasse in a gibbet camp. Defend the wagon of bells on the way back. Sister Pyx joins. | Bellfounder, Lychgates, Pyx |\n| 3 | **Saint Gloam's Bones** | Ossuary, 3 floors | Mother Ogive needs three relic bones. Under the third floor is the Counting House and **the Bursar**, who has a ledger with the Bishop's name in it. | Reliquary, sockets, first legendary guaranteed |\n| 4 | **The Cinder Cloister** | Burning abbey | Rescue the last novices. Malison's voice taunts you openly now. Confront **Bishop Ambrose**, who is buried and bursts out three times. The Crier reads the truth about the saint. | Passing the Belfry gate |\n| 5 | **Ring It or Die Trying** | The Cracked Belfry | Climb the tower. Fight **Malison** beneath the bell. Ring Old Clemency by hand. | Ending, credits, Penance, Knell Runs |\n\n**Ending:** the bell rings true, Malison comes apart, dawn hits the moor. Over the credits, far below, a much deeper bell answers. It's the hook for the rest of the game.\n\n**Side content:** the **Drowned Pantry** (optional small cellar off the Causeway, mini-boss the **Cellarer**, a \"return the overdue book\" joke quest for the Crier); 12 lore books (read aloud, kept in the Codex); **Writs** (section 7).\n\n### The first five minutes\n\n| Time | Beat |\n|---|---|\n| 0:00 | Title, New Hero, pick a class |\n| 0:10 | Narrator over black, a cracked bell toll; 20 seconds, skippable |\n| 0:30 | Night, rain, graveyard. A coffin bursts; hero plays `GetUp`. Prompt: move |\n| 0:40 | Three Peatwalkers. Prompt: attack. They burst. Urns and a barrel shatter, gold rains |\n| 1:30 | Tallow drops, the candle re-melts. A short-beam blue weapon drops. Toast: \"UPGRADE: [glyph] EQUIP\" |\n| 2:30 | Level 2, second skill unlocked, prompt to assign |\n| 3:00 | First telegraph: a red ring grows. Prompt: dodge |\n| 4:00 | An Unquiet Coffin at the gate. 45 seconds of chaos, a chest of gold, level 3 |\n| 5:00 | Tollhaven. \"Hear ye, hear ye!\" |\n\nPacing target: about 60 minutes to the credits for a first player. Tutorial 5, town 3, Causeway 12, Ossuary 15, town 2, Cloister 12, Belfry 8 with Malison, ending 2. A Knell Run takes 6 to 8 minutes.\n\n**New Hero** offers two starts: **The Beginning**, or **The Last Bell**: a level 30 hero with every skill and Tone unlocked, three legendaries, two pieces of its class set, materials and gems, 5 Devotion points to spend and a Grand Key, with the town open and Knell Runs available. Devotion needs level 30 and the story ends near level 20, so this start is what lets anyone see the whole endgame at once.\n\n### Where the player meets each feature\n\nTASK.md wants every feature shown, so none of them is left to luck. The playthrough tests in section 16 assert every row.\n\n| Feature | First met |\n|---|---|\n| Move, attack, dodge, potion, Tallow, gold, smashing props, telegraphs | St. Clement's Yard (tutorial) |\n| Item drops, rarity beams, one-button equip | Yard: the first upgrade is scripted |\n| Votive Shrine, Unquiet Coffin | Yard: both scripted |\n| Skill slots, Tones | Level 2 and level 3, with prompts |\n| Vows | Level 5 (first) and level 15 (second) |\n| Pennywhistle, Collection Box, Crier, Codex, How to Play, difficulty change | Tollhaven, after quest 1 |\n| Dame Brasse (salvage, craft, reforge), Lychgates, Passing Bell, Sister Pyx, Writs | Quest 2 |\n| Champion pack, the Collection Plate, an escort event, the side dungeon | Causeway: the first Plate and first pack are scripted |\n| Rare pack, traps and levers, gems and sockets, Mother Ogive (extract, Reliquary), first boss, first legendary | Ossuary |\n| The Unrung (demons), a defence event, second boss | Cloister |\n| Set items, final boss, ending | Belfry |\n| Penance tiers, Knell Runs | After the credits, or at once with The Last Bell |\n| Grand Knell, Devotion, set bonus | The Last Bell at once, or by playing on |\n| Couch co-op | Any time: a toast appears when a second pad is seen, and Party is in the pause menu |\n| AI Hub round | When the hub launches the game |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Combat\n\n### Rules\n\n- **Simulation:** 60 Hz in `fixedUpdate`, on a flat world (y = 0). Hero speed 5 to 5.6 m/s, radius 0.4 m. Bodies push softly; a hero stuck for 0.3 s passes through small monsters.\n- **Cast states:** wind-up, hit frame, recovery. Attack speed scales wind-up and recovery. Move input cancels the second half of recovery; dodge cancels anything. Hold a button to repeat.\n- **Moving while casting:** the Lamplighter's Spark Bolt and Sexton's Bone Toss play upper-body clips over `Run` (section 2, item 17). Everything else roots the hero for its wind-up.\n- **Dodge:** a 0.35 s `Roll`, 4.5 m, invulnerable throughout, 3 s cooldown (slider).\n- **Potion:** infinite, heals 60%, 30 s cooldown.\n- **Tallow:** kills drop it at 8% (elites always). Walking over it heals 20%.\n- **Gold** flies to you inside the pick-up radius (3 m). **Items** are picked up by walking over them (inventory has 40 slots; when full, they stay on the ground with a message).\n- **Telegraphs:** any attack worth more than 25% of a same-level hero's life shows a growing red ground ring or line for at least 0.6 s, with a warning sound. Dodging through it always works.\n- **Requiem:** kills within 1 second of each other chain. At 10+ a banner shows the count and pays bonus XP (count × 2% of the kill XP total).\n- **Death:** the candle goes out. Solo: 0.8 s of slow motion. Options: rise here (5 s wait, plus 5 s for each recent death, up to 30), last Lychgate, or town. No penalty","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-162042--x14746a-work/b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240/tool-results/bgv49cumv.txt","persistedOutputSize":123162},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AMMe1VFQhKGGXvA5iTqAwg","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke1ZipXrBLkXEdqj3ou","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0175JE4XovCJ2PQqaxNQMAeU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162042__x14746a/work/TASK.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":944,"cache_read_input_tokens":19148,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":944},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240","uuid":"3a700df4-263a-4f5d-8d05-1a682f7495a7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:47.673Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke1ZVvZpShsft7Qzm4A","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0175JE4XovCJ2PQqaxNQMAeU":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162042__x14746a/work/TASK.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0175JE4XovCJ2PQqaxNQMAeU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: Diablo 3\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## This computer\n33\tChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n34\t\n35\t- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n36\t- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n37\t- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n38\t- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n39\t  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n40\t  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n41\t  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n42\t- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n43\t- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n44\t- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n45\t- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n46\t- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n47\t\n48\t### What this computer doesn't have\n49\t\n50\tNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n51\t\n52\t- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n53\t- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n54\t- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n55\t- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n56\t- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n57\t- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n58\t\n59\t### Sharing this computer\n60\t\n61\t- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n62\t- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n63\t- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n64\t- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n65\t\n66\t## Goal\n67\t\n68\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n69\t\n70\tScope\n71\t\n72\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73\t\n74\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75\t\n76\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n77\t\n78\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n79\t\n80\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n81\t\n82\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n83\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n84\t\n85\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n86\t\n87\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n88\t\n89\t## Done\n90\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n91\t\n92\t## Source Information\n93\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n94\t- Date: 2026-11-05 16:20:42\n95\t- Source: the work it's based on\n96\t- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n97\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n98\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n99\t- Art Direction: style used\n100\t- Animation: system used\n101\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n102\t- Online: network features used, or none\n103\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n104\t- Research: research done, or none\n105\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n106\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n107\t- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n108\t\n109\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n110\t{\n111\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n112\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n113\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n114\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n115\t  \"aiHub\": {\n116\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n117\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n118\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n119\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n120\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n121\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n122\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n123\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n124\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n125\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n126\t  }\n127\t}\n128\tThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n129\t\n130\tTesting Feedback\n131\t\n132\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n133\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n134\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n135\t- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n136\t\n137\tEngine Feedback\n138\t\n139\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n140\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n141\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n142\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n143\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n144\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n145\t\n146\tPrompt Feedback\n147\t\n148\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n149\t- What was confusing?\n150\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n151\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240","uuid":"19f7a001-79d8-4e65-a727-36ec3218126f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:47.684Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162042__x14746a/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: Diablo 3\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-05 16:20:42\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","numLines":152,"startLine":1,"totalLines":152}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0175JE4XovCJ2PQqaxNQMAeU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke1ZipXrBLkXEdqj3ou","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018SwXAxERhZ6JccBFcHzLx4","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162042__x14746a/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":944,"cache_read_input_tokens":19148,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":944},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240","uuid":"d8111cba-b275-4624-a32e-082c45a16b7a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:47.854Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke1ZVvZpShsft7Qzm4A","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018SwXAxERhZ6JccBFcHzLx4":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162042__x14746a/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018SwXAxERhZ6JccBFcHzLx4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Game plan: a gothic action RPG for the TV, inspired by Diablo 3\n2\t\n3\t**Genre:** co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash loot game). **Camera:** fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic.\n4\t**Players:** 1 to 4, on one screen or in AI Hub sessions. **Engine:** the Pixel Engine kit (0.46.0 when this was written).\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## 0. How to use this plan\n9\t\n10\t- TASK.md is the CEO's brief and wins over this plan. This plan decides everything TASK.md leaves open, so you can build without asking.\n11\t- Read in this order: this plan, then the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md` whole, then the hub's `README.md` whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n12\t- **Build order is in section 15.** Follow it. The deliverable HTML must build and run from the first milestone on, and every milestone leaves the game playable.\n13\t- Lines marked **(verified)** were run on this computer against engine 0.46.0 in a throwaway build. Trust them. If your kit is newer, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first.\n14\t- Numbers are starting values. Anything about balance lives in one table (`src/data/tuning.js`) and shows up as a dev-mode slider.\n15\t- No more source research is needed. Section 1 says what to take from the source.\n16\t- All paths are relative to your dev folder. Your TASK.md gives the names and places of the dev folder, the deliverable and the exit interview file.\n17\t- **Other agents share this computer.** TASK.md's \"This computer\" notes apply as written, with one correction (section 2, item 5). Stay out of the folders beside yours. Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp`, which is shared. Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`; the browsers are already downloaded. Keep one or two browsers open at most. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n18\t- **No stubs.** If you run out of budget, cut whole features in the order given in section 15, never halfway. A feature that isn't there is fine; a menu item that does nothing is not.\n19\t\n20\t### What you hand back\n21\t\n22\t1. **One HTML file,** named by the pattern in your TASK.md and placed where it says, at the root above the dev folder. It holds the whole game: the engine, your code and the hub client, inlined. TASK.md allows CDN dependencies, but this game needs none.\n23\t2. **The exit interview file** beside that HTML, in the exact shape in section 17.\n24\t3. **Your final chat reply,** carrying Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback. The same four appear on the in-game About/Credits screen (section 13).\n25\t\n26\t**The bar:** a feature-complete demo, treated as a complete, polished vertical slice, long enough to show every feature and mechanic so a player understands how the game plays and feels and what they can and can't do. It carries all the infrastructure the rest of the game would need. No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features.\n27\t\n28\t### The title\n29\t\n30\t**Pick it first:** the names of your dev folder and your HTML include it. As TASK.md says: a playful riff on the source's name; silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator by running code. Make it a pun that reads as its own name, not the source's name with a letter changed. It lives in one constant, `TITLE` in `src/data/meta.js`, and appears only in the game's frame: title screen, attract overlay, session card, About, the no-WebGPU page, `<title>`, the engine's game `name` and the save key. Story text never mentions it, so the world below works with any title.\n31\t\n32\t---\n33\t\n34\t## 1. The game\n35\t\n36\t### Pitch\n37\t\n38\tForty days ago the great bell that kept the dead asleep cracked. It still tolls every dusk, but now it says *get up*, and everything buried does. You got up too, except that you kept your wits. Pick a trade (swing a holy bell on a chain, hurl lanterns, or bury your problems with a shovel), smash hordes that burst apart, chase loot that rings like church bells, and climb the broken tower to silence the walled-up saint who has been screaming at you the whole way. Alone, on a couch, or online.\n39\t\n40\t### Pillars (when in doubt, these decide)\n41\t\n42\t1. **Every button press breaks something.** Hits have weight: flash, shake, knockback, ragdolls, shattered props. Hordes are the point.\n43\t2. **Loot you can hear.** Rarity is a colour, a beam and a sound. Legendary drops ring a bell. Good items change how skills work, not only the numbers.\n44\t3. **Builds are play styles.** Six slots, eight skills per class, three variations of each. Swapping is free and instant, so experimenting costs nothing.\n45\t4. **Gothic melodrama with a straight face and a black joke.** Villains proclaim. Townsfolk shrug. Nobody winks at the camera.\n46\t5. **A TV game.** Big, readable, one screen at a time, playable from a sofa with a pad. Mouse and keyboard feel native on the same screens.\n47\t6. **One more run.** After the story, a repeatable run mode with a meter, a boss and rising difficulty.\n48\t\n49\t### What we take from the source, and what we make ours\n50\t\n51\tTASK.md asks you to capture what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, and turn it into mechanics, pacing and UX. So the first rows are the feel of the thing, made ours.\n52\t\n53\t| What the source does well | Our version |\n54\t|---|---|\n55\t| A gothic land where hell climbs up through its graveyards, and a doomed town holding out | **Vespermoor** and **Tollhaven** |\n56\t| A hero who is more than mortal | **The Late**: dead, up again, and still themselves |\n57\t| A grim old keeper of lore who reads you the history | **Crier Maudlin**, a severed head who never stopped working |\n58\t| Themes of apocalypse, failing faith and old sins coming due | A cracked holy bell, a wronged saint, a bishop in debt |\n59\t| Hordes that explode, physical destruction | Ragdolls, shatter props, a kill-streak bonus called **Requiem** |\n60\t| Classes with their own resource | **Bellwarden** (Clangor), **Lamplighter** (Wick), **Sexton** (Tithe) |\n61\t| Skills with swappable modifiers | 8 skills per class, each with 3 **Tones** |\n62\t| Passive choices | 4 **Vows** per class, pick 2 |\n63\t| Life pickups that drop in combat | **Tallow**: wax drops that re-melt your life candle |\n64\t| Beams and sounds for great loot | Coloured beams, and a bell strike for every legendary |\n65\t| Elites with random powers | Elite packs with **Afflictions** |\n66\t| The loot creature you chase | **The Collection Plate**: a living offering dish on little legs, spilling coins as it runs |\n67\t| Risky chests | **Unquiet Coffins**: open one, survive 45 seconds, get paid |\n68\t| Temporary shrines | **Votive Shrines** |\n69\t| Repeatable random dungeon with a meter and a boss | **Knell Runs**; the timed version is the **Grand Knell** |\n70\t| Endless levelling | **Devotion** |\n71\t| Difficulty ladder | **Pilgrim, Zealot, Martyr, Penance I to V** |\n72\t| Crafting artisans | **Dame Brasse** the bellfounder, **Mother Ogive** at the Reliquary |\n73\t| Extracting item powers | The **Reliquary** holds three extracted powers |\n74\t| Waypoints, town portal | **Lychgates**, the **Passing Bell** |\n75\t| An AI companion | **Sister Pyx** |\n76\t| Couch and online co-op | Couch co-op on its own, shared-world sessions in AI Hub |\n77\t| Scheming lords of the pit who taunt you while you play | **The Bursar** and **Malison**, in subtitled gibberish |\n78\t\n79\t### Stay original\n80\t\n81\tThis is a new game in the same genre, with its own world, cast, art, music and levels. TASK.md says so and it's a hard rule.\n82\t\n83\t- Never use the source's names or look: Sanctuary, Tristram, Caldeum, Westmarch, Horadrim, Nephalem, Cain, Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Imperius, Malthael, Azmodan, Belial, Kanai, Paragon, Torment, its class names and resource names, its famous lines, its melodies, or a HUD built around two orbs. No fallen star, no amnesiac angel.\n84\t- The source is credited by name only on the About screen and in the exit interview.\n85\t- `tools/test.mjs` greps `src/` for these names as whole words (section 16).\n86\t\n87\t---\n88\t\n89\t## 2. Facts verified on this computer\n90\t\n91\tThese save you hours. All were run in a real build here.\n92\t\n93\t**Bundling**\n94\t1. **(verified)** One HTML file boots on `file://` with WebGPU. Bundle with `esbuild game.js --bundle --format=esm --target=es2022 --charset=utf8 --legal-comments=none`, put the result in one inline `<script type=\"module\">`. The engine has no `import.meta` and needs no fetch, so its built-in models and Rapier's wasm work from the file. The result is about 5.3 MB plus your code, with nothing external. Don't minify your own code: the agent that imports the game adds its usage figures to the About screen later, so keep strings findable.\n95\t2. Escape `</script` as `<\\/script` in the bundle. If you insert with `String.replace`, use a function replacer, or `$&` in the code corrupts the file.\n96\t3. **(verified)** Page order that the hub accepts: `<meta charset>`, then `session.js` byte for byte as the first script, then the `hub:*` metas, then `<title>`. Body: `<div id=\"app\"></div>` and the module script.\n97\t4. **(verified)** If you ever need a pack, set `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__['packs/x.json'] = 'data:application/json;base64,...'` in a classic script before the module, then `loadPack('packs/x.json')` works on `file://`. The built-in library is enough for this game, so prefer not to.\n98\t\n99\t**Browsers in tests**\n100\t5. **(verified) Use the kit's flags:** `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` (the list in `check.mjs`). The shorter list in the \"This computer\" notes lost the GPU device within seconds on this engine (`A valid external Instance reference no longer exists`). That error is the flags, not your game.\n101\t6. **(verified)** Software rendering is slow: about 20 fps for a small scene at 1280×720, 3 to 8 fps at 1080p with 40 rigged monsters. So tests advance time with `engine.step(n)` (120 frames took 6 ms in a small scene) and read pixels with `await engine.renderer.capture()` (about 180 ms). Never wait in real time when you can step.\n102\t7. **(verified)** `Engine.headless` works in Node with `buildModel`, `compileClips`, mixers, HUD calls and audio counts. `hud.width` is 480 and `hud.canvas` is null there. `node check.mjs --headless game.js --keys KeyD*30` plays and replays to the same fingerprint. Replays still match with ragdolls and flying chunks in play. Every module must guard `window`, `document` and `navigator` with `typeof`.\n103\t\n104\t**The hub**\n105\t8. **(verified)** A full engine in the harness's iframes passes: 2 players; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; the same with `scenario=host-leave`; 2 players with `scenario=server`; and a game-owned card. Copies were ready in 2 to 6.5 seconds.\n106\t9. **(verified)** For `AIHub.server` (a copy with no player) use `Engine.headless(game, {})` inside the iframe and drive it with a timer: ready in 0.6 s, no GPU, harness passes. Step by elapsed time, not a fixed count.\n107\t10. **(verified)** On the game's own card, answer Enter from a plain `window` `keydown` listener, not `wasPressed`. The harness allows 3 seconds and software-rendered frames are slow.\n108\t11. The hub's clocks (from `round.js`): hard limit is `hub:session-minutes` × 1.5, at most 15 minutes; 5 s grace after `time-up`; 3 s countdown; 20 s load grace; 20 s hidden-host limit.\n109\t\n110\t**Characters and animation**\n111\t12. **(verified)** You can build any humanoid as a `ModelDef` of nested pivots named after `HERO_RIG`'s joints, and `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, build.object)` animates it with all 78 hero clips, including `Slash1`, `Slash2`, `Slash3`, `Slam`, `LeapSlam`, `Spin`, `Cast`, `CastBig`, `CastWeapon`, `CastBigWeapon`, `BowDraw`, `BowRelease`, `Roll`, `Charge`, `Shout`, `HitReact`, `Death`, `Triumph`. `new Ragdoll(physics, root, HERO_RAGDOLL)` works on the same body. Template in section 8.\n112\t13. **(verified)** All 15 joints must exist or `compileClips` throws `rig joint \"ForearmR\" not found`. An empty pivot is fine, which keeps cheap monsters cheap (14 meshes against 21).\n113\t14. **(verified)** Compile clips once per body type (63 ms for 78 clips) and share them: `new THREE.AnimationMixer(instance).clipAction(clip)` moves a second instance. `buildModel` is cheap and shares geometry.\n114\t15. **(verified)** Custom clips: `{ name, frames, fast, keys: [[frame, { ArmR: [x, y, z], Pelvis: { p: [x, y, z] } }, 'outCubic'], ...] }` and `gaitClip(HERO_RIG, {...})` compile with `validateClip` clean.\n115\t16. **(verified)** `analyzeClip` throws on merged pivot bodies because its sole meshes are gone. Run it on the engine's own hero inside `Engine.headless`: `const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL)`, same rig, same results. It finds real problems such as `planted foot slides 0.80 m/s`; tune `speed` and `stance` until it's clean.\n116\t17. **(verified)** To attack while running, filter a compiled clip to upper-body tracks: `new THREE.AnimationClip(name, clip.duration, clip.tracks.filter(t => UPPER.has(t.name.split('.')[0])))` and play it over `Run`. Track names look like `Torso.quaternion`. It ran without error; check how it looks.\n117\t\n118\t**Pause, input, camera, HUD**\n119\t18. **(verified) Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`.** `update` keeps running with `dt` 0, `fixedUpdate` stops, `wasPressed` still works, so menus work. `engine.paused = true` stops `update` too: never use it for menus. Menu animation counts rendered frames, since `dt` is 0.\n120\t19. `hitstop` and `timeScale` stall the whole simulation. Never use them on a session host. Use them solo and on the couch only.\n121\t20. **(verified)** Use `aspect: 'fill'`. The default letterboxes (at 1280×720 you get 960×540 in the middle). With `fill` the art size changes with the window: 427×240 at 720p, 480×270 at 1080p and 4K, 512×288 at 1440p, about 136×276 on a portrait phone. Lay out from `hud.width` and `hud.height`, never from 480×270.\n122\t21. **(verified)** The `iso` camera with yaw 45 looks toward −X −Z: screen right is (+0.71, 0, −0.71), screen up is (−0.71, 0, −0.71). So a wall on a room's −X or −Z side shows its face to the camera, and a wall on the +X or +Z side blocks the view.\n123\t22. **(verified)** `camera: { preset: 'iso', viewHeight: 11.5, pitch: 42, yaw: 45 }` looks right at 1080p: a hero about 25 art pixels tall, with 40 monsters on screen and still readable. A `viewHeight` of 13 still reads, which gives the room to zoom out for co-op.\n124\t23. **(verified)** The rig zooms on the wheel after `update` runs. Read `ctx.input.wheel` yourself, then set `ctx.input.wheel = 0` to stop it. `setZoom` clamps to `minZoom` and `maxZoom`.\n125\t24. **(verified)** `hud.text` uppercases for you and draws `?` for any character it lacks. It has digits, A to Z, straight and curly quotes, the backtick and `. , : ; - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ ! ? < > # * @ ° · & | ~ ^ $`. No em dash, no ×, no arrows. Text is 6 art pixels per character (minus one) and 7 tall. `hudSprite` icons are about 12×12. A test lints all strings against this set.\n126\t25. **(verified)** The engine's `Input` merges every pad into one, which can't do couch co-op. Set `ctx.input.gamepadButtons = {}` and read `navigator.getGamepads()` yourself, per pad (guard with `typeof`). A stub via `addInitScript` reaches both. The kit's pad mapping is for the platformer and would press Space, KeyJ and so on.\n127\t26. Mouse buttons are keys (`MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`), and the canvas already blocks the context menu. The HUD canvas has `pointer-events: none`, so the pointer reaches the game through it.\n128\t\n129\t**Audio**\n130\t27. **(verified)** Engine defaults are master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, saved under `pixel-engine:audio`, a key shared by every engine game on the same origin. So on first run (your own flag in storage) call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. `audio.resetVolumes()`, which the engine's settings panel calls, puts it back to 0.6, so wrap that method on the instance to re-apply 0.1. That doesn't touch `pixel-engine.js`.\n131\t\n132\t**Touch and look**\n133\t28. **(verified)** Detect touch-only with `!matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` (true on Chromium's Pixel 7 profile, false on desktop). `touch: false` removes the joystick. The ⚙ button stays visible. WebKit here has no WebGPU, so test touch in Chromium.\n134\t29. **(verified) The mood:** `engine.sun.color.setHex(PALETTE.sky); engine.sun.intensity = 0.55; engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy); engine.ambient.intensity = 0.9; scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.ink);` plus library torches and a warm light following the hero gives a rich dark dungeon. A tall thin `glowMaterial` box plus a coloured point light on the floor makes a superb loot beam.\n135\t30. **(verified)** Floor textures at full white are too loud against monsters. `toonMaterial(PALETTE.mist, { map })` pushes them back and monsters read much better; `flagstones` tinted that way looked best, and `ashlar` is busy, so keep it for walls. Bright red `Decals` at 1 to 2 m look cartoonish; `plum` at 0.4 to 0.9 m sits right.\n136\t\n137\t**Randomness**\n138\t31. **(verified) `Rng.fork` depends on the parent's current state,** whatever the docs' comment suggests: once the parent has drawn a number, the same `fork('zone:1')` gives different numbers. So keep one root `new Rng(seed)` per world that only forks and never draws. Forks from that root are stable, whatever their children draw. Every session client relies on this to build the same map.\n139\t\n140\t**Also run under `Engine.headless` and working (verified):** `Breakables` (`add`, then `break`); `Timeline` (cues, `line()`, `bars()`, `skip()`); `NavGrid` (`flowTo`, `flowDirection`, `path`, `lineOfSight`, `setOpen`); `LegStepper` with `twoBoneIK`; `SpringChain`; `Squash`; `Decals`; `Trail`; `Precipitation`; `WaterSurface`; `groundFog`; `{ dissolve: true }` clones; `glowMaterial` swaps; `mergeStaticMeshes` with a tinted `textureMap`; `disposeObject` on a zone root; `camera.rayAt` onto the ground; `lights.request` with `lifetime` and `priority`; `screen.cover`, `reveal`, `shockwave` and `flash`; `input.send` reaching `input.commands`; `audio.loop` with `spatialize`; the bell as sine `layers` (peak 0.84 from `renderSound`); `parseSong` on the Clemency motif. These ran without error; how they look is yours to check.\n141\t\n142\t**This engine start worked (verified):**\n143\t\n144\t```js\n145\tconst engine = await Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({\n146\t  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n147\t  camera: { preset: 'iso', viewHeight: 11.5, pitch: 42, yaw: 45, minZoom: 0.6, maxZoom: 1.4 },\n148\t  aspect: 'fill', quality: 'high', touch: false,\n149\t}));\n150\t```\n151\t\n152\tAdd `settings: { button: touchOnly ? 'top-right' : false }` to that **(verified: `false` removes the button)**. Your own Settings screen replaces the ⚙ everywhere except touch-only devices, where TASK.md asks for it.\n153\t\n154\t---\n155\t\n156\t## 3. World, story and tone\n157\t\n158\t### The world\n159\t\n160\t**Vespermoor** is a drowned moorland kingdom under one enormous tower, the **Belfry of Saint Malison**. In it hangs the Great Bell, **Old Clemency**. For a thousand years it tolled at dusk, and the toll told everything below: *stay down*. Forty days ago it cracked. It still tolls. It now says something else.\n161\t\n162\t- **The Risen:** the mindless dead, up and about.\n163\t- **The Unrung:** demons from Below, things no bell was ever rung for. They hate sound, and they've come up through the graves the dead left open.\n164\t- **The Late (formally the Tollborn):** you. You died during the cracked toll, or just after, and got up with your mind intact. Townsfolk find this awkward. They call you *the Late* and ask you to wipe your feet. Death doesn't stick to you, which is why you respawn.\n165\t- **Tollhaven:** the last parish with its lamps lit.\n166\t\n167\t**The truth:** Saint Malison, revered as the foundress who \"gave herself to the bell\", was walled up alive in the tower's foundation by the first bishops, singing. She has heard that bell every dusk for a thousand years. Bishop Ambrose, drowning in debts to a demon called the Bursar, cracked it for her. She wants one thing: to ring the Last Toll, **Eventide**, and wake everything ever buried. The only thing she still fears is the sound of Old Clemency rung true.\n168\t\n169\t### Cast\n170\t\n171\t| Who | Role | Voice and manner |\n172\t|---|---|---|\n173\t| **Crier Maudlin** | Quest giver, reads lore aloud. A severed head in a lantern on a post. Lost the body, kept the job. | High, reedy, unstoppable |\n174\t| **Dame Brasse** | Bellfounder: salvage, craft, reforge. Melts church bells into swords. Half deaf. | LOUD. Everything shouted |\n175\t| **Mother Ogive** | Keeper of the Reliquary: extract powers, sockets. Blind; knows relics by taste. | Soft, delighted, unsettling |\n176\t| **Pennywhistle** | Merchant in a plague-doctor beak. | Cheerful, nasal, modest markups considering the apocalypse |\n177\t| **Sister Pyx** | Companion. Armoured nun with a crossbow. | Deadpan |\n178\t| **Bishop Ambrose Thrice-Buried** | Kindly, pale, keeps a spade in the coffin. The traitor. | Warm, then not |\n179\t| **The Bursar** | Mid-game boss. A bloated demon accountant in a stolen cope. | Booming, delighted by interest |\n180\t| **Malison, the Unrung Saint** | Final boss. Huge entombed saint wrapped in bell ropes. | Vast, layered, furious |\n181\t| **The Narrator** | Opening and ending. | Deep and slow |\n182\t\n183\t### Tone\n184\t\n185\tThree registers, and write everything in one of them:\n186\t\n187\t1. **High gothic proclamation:** narration, lore books, Malison. Long vowels, doom, an ominous last line.\n188\t2. **Gallows humour:** townsfolk, item flavour, death messages. Dry, never modern. No slang, no winks.\n189\t3. **Boss taunts:** direct address, mid-fight, short.\n190\t\n191\tSubtitles fit two rows of about 60 characters, which every line below does. Lore books get a paged screen instead. Punctuation stays inside the HUD font (section 2, item 24). Use these as written and write the rest to match.\n192\t\n193\t**Narrator:** \"For a thousand years Old Clemency tolled at dusk, and the dead of Vespermoor stayed down.\" / \"Forty days ago the Great Bell cracked. It still tolls. It now says something else.\" / \"You were buried on the thirty-ninth day. It did not take.\"\n194\t\n195\t**Crier Maudlin:** \"Hear ye, hear ye! The dead are up, the roads are closed, and the fish is off. Also you. You are dead. Welcome back.\" / \"I lost the body at harvest and kept the job. No one told me to stop.\" / \"The parish asks that you wipe your feet. You have been in the ground.\"\n196\t\n197\t**Dame Brasse:** \"WHAT? SPEAK UP! I MELT BELLS FOR A LIVING!\" / \"THAT SWORD WAS A CHURCH BELL ON TUESDAY. HIT SOMETHING WITH IT AND IT STILL SAYS AMEN.\"\n198\t\n199\t**Mother Ogive:** \"Bring me the holy bones, child. I will know them by the taste.\" / \"A saint's knuckle. Very old. A little salty. She died angry.\"\n200\t\n201\t**Pennywhistle:** \"Everything is half price, dear friend. The other half is your soul, but we can discuss terms.\" / \"No refunds. No returns. No survivors, so far.\"\n202\t\n203\t**Sister Pyx:** \"I have killed four hundred things today and confessed to none of them.\" / \"Bless you. Duck.\" / \"You smell like a grave. It is an improvement on the last one.\"\n204\t\n205\t**Bishop Ambrose:** \"They have buried me three times, my child. One learns to keep a spade in the coffin.\"\n206\t\n207\t**The Bursar:** \"YOUR SOUL IS OVERDUE!\" / \"INTEREST COMPOUNDS, LITTLE CORPSE!\" / \"I HAVE YOU DOWN FOR ONE DEATH. I SEE IT WAS NOT COLLECTED.\" / \"FORECLOSURE!\"\n208\t\n209\t**Malison:** \"A THOUSAND YEARS I HAVE BEEN YOUR FOUNDATION STONE. NOW I SHALL BE YOUR CEILING.\" / \"THEY WALLED ME UP SINGING. DO YOU KNOW WHAT I LEARNED IN THE DARK? EVERY VERSE.\" / \"RUN, LITTLE RINGER. THE HOUR IS UNRUNG, AND IT IS YOURS.\" / \"LISTEN. THAT IS EVERY GRAVE YOU EVER WALKED ON, OPENING.\" / (on a hero's death) \"DOWN YOU GO. GIVE MY REGARDS TO THE WORMS.\"\n210\t\n211\t**Lore book:** \"THE THIRD NIGHT. THE GRAVES IN THE NEW YARD ARE EMPTY. THE GRAVES IN THE OLD YARD ARE EMPTY. I HAVE BEGUN DIGGING ONE FOR MYSELF, FOR THE COMPANY.\"\n212\t\n213\t**System lines:** Death: \"YOU HAVE DIED. AGAIN. IT IS BECOMING A HABIT.\" Level up: \"YOU FEEL MORE ALIVE. THIS IS NOT MEDICALLY POSSIBLE.\" Streak: \"37 KILL REQUIEM\".\n214\t\n215\t**Item flavour:** \"She rang it only once. It was enough.\" / \"The best one is still in him.\" / \"Smells of smoke and regret.\"\n216\t\n217\t### The story, in five quests\n218\t\n219\t| # | Quest | Where | What happens | Unlocks |\n220\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n221\t| 1 | **Wipe Your Feet** | St. Clement's Yard | Your coffin bursts. Tutorial fights. The Crier's voice carries over the wall. Reach Tollhaven. | Town, Pennywhistle, potion |\n222\t| 2 | **The Weeping Causeway** | The Causeway | Find Dame Brasse in a gibbet camp. Defend the wagon of bells on the way back. Sister Pyx joins. | Bellfounder, Lychgates, Pyx |\n223\t| 3 | **Saint Gloam's Bones** | Ossuary, 3 floors | Mother Ogive needs three relic bones. Under the third floor is the Counting House and **the Bursar**, who has a ledger with the Bishop's name in it. | Reliquary, sockets, first legendary guaranteed |\n224\t| 4 | **The Cinder Cloister** | Burning abbey | Rescue the last novices. Malison's voice taunts you openly now. Confront **Bishop Ambrose**, who is buried and bursts out three times. The Crier reads the truth about the saint. | Passing the Belfry gate |\n225\t| 5 | **Ring It or Die Trying** | The Cracked Belfry | Climb the tower. Fight **Malison** beneath the bell. Ring Old Clemency by hand. | Ending, credits, Penance, Knell Runs |\n226\t\n227\t**Ending:** the bell rings true, Malison comes apart, dawn hits the moor. Over the credits, far below, a much deeper bell answers. It's the hook for the rest of the game.\n228\t\n229\t**Side content:** the **Drowned Pantry** (optional small cellar off the Causeway, mini-boss the **Cellarer**, a \"return the overdue book\" joke quest for the Crier); 12 lore books (read aloud, kept in the Codex); **Writs** (section 7).\n230\t\n231\t### The first five minutes\n232\t\n233\t| Time | Beat |\n234\t|---|---|\n235\t| 0:00 | Title, New Hero, pick a class |\n236\t| 0:10 | Narrator over black, a cracked bell toll; 20 seconds, skippable |\n237\t| 0:30 | Night, rain, graveyard. A coffin bursts; hero plays `GetUp`. Prompt: move |\n238\t| 0:40 | Three Peatwalkers. Prompt: attack. They burst. Urns and a barrel shatter, gold rains |\n239\t| 1:30 | Tallow drops, the candle re-melts. A short-beam blue weapon drops. Toast: \"UPGRADE: [glyph] EQUIP\" |\n240\t| 2:30 | Level 2, second skill unlocked, prompt to assign |\n241\t| 3:00 | First telegraph: a red ring grows. Prompt: dodge |\n242\t| 4:00 | An Unquiet Coffin at the gate. 45 seconds of chaos, a chest of gold, level 3 |\n243\t| 5:00 | Tollhaven. \"Hear ye, hear ye!\" |\n244\t\n245\tPacing target: about 60 minutes to the credits for a first player. Tutorial 5, town 3, Causeway 12, Ossuary 15, town 2, Cloister 12, Belfry 8 with Malison, ending 2. A Knell Run takes 6 to 8 minutes.\n246\t\n247\t**New Hero** offers two starts: **The Beginning**, or **The Last Bell**: a level 30 hero with every skill and Tone unlocked, three legendaries, two pieces of its class set, materials and gems, 5 Devotion points to spend and a Grand Key, with the town open and Knell Runs available. Devotion needs level 30 and the story ends near level 20, so this start is what lets anyone see the whole endgame at once.\n248\t\n249\t### Where the player meets each feature\n250\t\n251\tTASK.md wants every feature shown, so none of them is left to luck. The playthrough tests in section 16 assert every row.\n252\t\n253\t| Feature | First met |\n254\t|---|---|\n255\t| Move, attack, dodge, potion, Tallow, gold, smashing props, telegraphs | St. Clement's Yard (tutorial) |\n256\t| Item drops, rarity beams, one-button equip | Yard: the first upgrade is scripted |\n257\t| Votive Shrine, Unquiet Coffin | Yard: both scripted |\n258\t| Skill slots, Tones | Level 2 and level 3, with prompts |\n259\t| Vows | Level 5 (first) and level 15 (second) |\n260\t| Pennywhistle, Collection Box, Crier, Codex, How to Play, difficulty change | Tollhaven, after quest 1 |\n261\t| Dame Brasse (salvage, craft, reforge), Lychgates, Passing Bell, Sister Pyx, Writs | Quest 2 |\n262\t| Champion pack, the Collection Plate, an escort event, the side dungeon | Causeway: the first Plate and first pack are scripted |\n263\t| Rare pack, traps and levers, gems and sockets, Mother Ogive (extract, Reliquary), first boss, first legendary | Ossuary |\n264\t| The Unrung (demons), a defence event, second boss | Cloister |\n265\t| Set items, final boss, ending | Belfry |\n266\t| Penance tiers, Knell Runs | After the credits, or at once with The Last Bell |\n267\t| Grand Knell, Devotion, set bonus | The Last Bell at once, or by playing on |\n268\t| Couch co-op | Any time: a toast appears when a second pad is seen, and Party is in the pause menu |\n269\t| AI Hub round | When the hub launches the game |\n270\t\n271\t---\n272\t\n273\t## 4. Combat\n274\t\n275\t### Rules\n276\t\n277\t- **Simulation:** 60 Hz in `fixedUpdate`, on a flat world (y = 0). Hero speed 5 to 5.6 m/s, radius 0.4 m. Bodies push softly; a hero stuck for 0.3 s passes through small monsters.\n278\t- **Cast states:** wind-up, hit frame, recovery. Attack speed scales wind-up and recovery. Move input cancels the second half of recovery; dodge cancels anything. Hold a button to repeat.\n279\t- **Moving while casting:** the Lamplighter's Spark Bolt and Sexton's Bone Toss play upper-body clips over `Run` (section 2, item 17). Everything else roots the hero for its wind-up.\n280\t- **Dodge:** a 0.35 s `Roll`, 4.5 m, invulnerable throughout, 3 s cooldown (slider).\n281\t- **Potion:** infinite, heals 60%, 30 s cooldown.\n282\t- **Tallow:** kills drop it at 8% (elites always). Walking over it heals 20%.\n283\t- **Gold** flies to you inside the pick-up radius (3 m). **Items** are picked up by walking over them (inventory has 40 slots; when full, they stay on the ground with a message).\n284\t- **Telegraphs:** any attack worth more than 25% of a same-level hero's life shows a growing red ground ring or line for at least 0.6 s, with a warning sound. Dodging through it always works.\n285\t- **Requiem:** kills within 1 second of each other chain. At 10+ a banner shows the count and pays bonus XP (count × 2% of the kill XP total).\n286\t- **Death:** the candle goes out. Solo: 0.8 s of slow motion. Options: rise here (5 s wait, plus 5 s for each recent death, up to 30), last Lychgate, or town. No penalty beyond the walk. In co-op an ally revives you by holding interact for 2 s.\n287\t\n288\t### Feel recipe (apply to every hit)\n289\t\n290\tUse all of these and tune with the \"feel\" slider.\n291\t\n292\t- Target flashes: swap `mesh.material` to a shared `glowMaterial(PALETTE.white)` for 2 frames. Don't clone materials per monster.\n293\t- Knockback slide; `ctx.particles.burst` tinted by element; a pitched-up hit sound; a damage number that pops and drifts. Crits use scale 2 and `sand`.\n294\t- Big hits: `engine.shake.add(0.1 to 0.5)`, `engine.screen.shockwave(at, { radius })` on novas, `hitstop(0.04 to 0.08)` when solo or on the couch only.\n295\t- **Kills:** humanoids go into `Ragdoll` with velocity away from the hit, scaled by overkill. Keep up to 16 live ragdolls; older ones freeze and dissolve (`dissolve: true` on a per-body clone at death only). Others squash, burst and dissolve. `Decals`: small `plum` splats and `scorch`.\n296\t- **Props:** urns, barrels, crates, coffins, tombstones have HP. On break, hide the library model and do `breaks.break(breaks.add({ at, size, color, cuts }), hitAt, speed)` so chunks fly. They drop gold 40% and Tallow 5%.\n297\t- **Elements:** Sound (`sand`, `white`, expanding rings), Flame (`orange`, `red`, `sand`, a warm point light), Grave (`lime`, `plum`, smoke, a green point light). Lights use `lifetime` and priority 3; heroes 4, loot 2, torches 1.\n298\t\n299\t### Targeting\n300\t\n301\t- **Pad:** skills target softly. The best monster inside a 120° cone of facing and the skill's range gets a reticle ring. Right stick overrides aim and moves the ground reticle for ground-target skills. Melee gets a 0.6 m lunge assist.\n302\t- **Mouse:** hero faces the pointer. Left click on a monster walks into range and attacks; on an NPC or object, walks and uses it; on the ground, moves (hold to keep steering). Shift-click attacks in place. `camera.rayAt` gives the ground point (solve for y = 0); pick monsters by screen distance to their chest (14 art pixels), items by their label rectangle.\n303\t- **Keyboard:** WASD or arrows also move.\n304\t\n305\t### Classes\n306\t\n307\tEveryone has 6 slots. Slots open at levels 1, 1, 3, 6, 9, 12. Any skill can go in any slot. Swap freely out of combat (no cost, 1 s lockout on the changed slot). Each skill has an unlock level; its three Tones unlock at +2, +6 and +11 levels after it (capped at 30). The first Vow opens at level 5, the second at level 15.\n308\t\n309\tPercentages are of weapon damage. \"Generator\" means it gives resource and costs nothing. Clips are engine clips unless marked *(new)*.\n310\t\n311\t#### The Bellwarden: melee bruiser\n312\t\n313\tA guardian of the bell-ringers' order who swings a consecrated hand-bell the size of an anvil on a chain. High life, armour +30%, speed 5.0.\n314\t\n315\t**Clangor** (0 to 100) builds on hits and drains after 4 s out of combat.\n316\t**Resonance:** every hit adds a stack to the monster (max 5, 6 s). *Toll* skills consume stacks for +25% damage each. Show stacks as small rings on the monster.\n317\t\n318\t| Skill (level) | Cost / cooldown | Clip | Effect | Tones |\n319\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n320\t| **Clout** (1) | Generator +10 | `Slash1`, `Slash2`, `Slash3` chain | 150%, 120° arc, 2.8 m | *Ringing:* chains 60% to 2 more. *Heavy:* slower, 230%, stun 0.4 s. *Pious:* heal 2% per target |\n321\t| **Chain Reap** (2) | Generator +6 per target | `Spin` once | 110% circle, 4 m | *Drag:* pulls 1.5 m. *Long Chain:* 5.5 m. *Rust:* bleed 40%/s for 3 s |\n322\t| **Great Toll** (4) | 30 Clangor, Toll | `BellOverhead` *(new)* | 300% ring, 6 m, knockback 3 m | *Twin Peal:* second ring after 0.5 s at 70%. *Cracked:* leaves shock patches 4 s. *Deaf:* 200%, stun 1 s |\n323\t| **Reverberate** (6) | 20 Clangor, Toll | `Shout` | 260% wave, cone, 9 m, passes through | *Echo:* bounces off walls once. *Narrow:* 60% narrower, 420%. *Hymn:* +3 Clangor per target |\n324\t| **Bellfall** (9) | 10 s | `LeapSlam` | Leap up to 12 m; 400%, 2 m, a bell falls with you | *Iron Bell:* leaves a taunting bell 5 s, 100%/s. *Swift:* 6 s, 250%. *Comet:* burning trail |\n325\t| **Clapper Charge** (12) | 8 s | `Charge` | Dash 10 m, 200%, shoves aside | *Battering:* stun at end. *Relentless:* kills refund 50% cooldown. *Wake:* sound trail 80%/s |\n326\t| **Hallowed Ground** (16) | 20 s | `CastBigWeapon` | 8 s ring, 7 m: allies take 30% less, heal 3%/s | *Vigil:* immune to stuns. *Ire:* +25% damage. *Procession:* follows you |\n327\t| **Last Peal** (20) | 45 s | `Spin` loop | 8 s spin: 180% per 0.25 s, 4.5 m, 20% slower, unstoppable | *Cyclone:* pulls. *Thunder:* ends with 800%. *Eternal:* kills add 0.3 s |\n328\t\n329\t**Vows (pick 2):** *Iron* (+20% armour). *Clamour* (+20 Clangor, no drain for 8 s). *Contrition* (+15% damage under half life). *Silence* (kills give +15% speed for 3 s).\n330\t\n331\t#### The Lamplighter: ranged kiter\n332\t\n333\tLast of the guild that keeps night at bay. Long coat, wide hat, a lamp pole on the back. Medium life, speed 5.6.\n334\t\n335\t**Wick** (0 to 100) regenerates 14/s.\n336\t**Kindle:** Flame hits Burn (30%/s for 4 s). Burning enemies take +15% from your skills.\n337\t\n338\t| Skill (level) | Cost / cooldown | Clip | Effect | Tones |\n339\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n340\t| **Spark Bolt** (1) | Generator +4 | `CastWeapon` upper | 120% fast bolt, 18 m, Burns | *Split:* pierce 1, forks on kill. *Swift:* 1.4× speed, 90%. *Star:* arcs to 2 more |\n341\t| **Flare** (2) | 20 Wick | `Cast` | 220% cone, 8 m, monsters miss for 1.5 s | *Phosphor:* 50% wider. *Magnesium:* 340%, no blind. *Signal:* marks, allies +20% for 5 s |\n342\t| **Lantern Hurl** (4) | 30 Wick | `LanternLob` *(new)* | Lob up to 14 m: 240% in 3 m, burning pool 4 s at 60%/s | *Cluster:* 3 small. *Oil:* 8 s pool. *Blue Flame:* 40% slow |\n343\t| **Lamp Post** (6) | 12 s, max 3 | `Dig` *(new)* | Plants a lamp 15 s, fires 70% bolts twice a second | *Arc Lamp:* beams between lamps. *Bright:* heals allies in 4 m. *Bomb:* explodes 500% at the end |\n344\t| **Dusk Step** (9) | 6 s | `Roll` | Blink 8 m, ember decoy 2 s | *Double:* 2 charges. *Scorch:* 180% blast at origin. *Veil:* 2 s unseen, next hit +100% |\n345\t| **Snuff** (12) | 40 Wick | `CastBig` | All Burn within 14 m pays out at once: remaining burn × 3 | *Smother:* stun 1 s. *Relight:* re-Burns after 2 s. *Harvest:* +8 Wick per target |\n346\t| **Beacon** (16) | 25 s | `CastBigWeapon` | 10 s pillar, 7 m: allies +20% damage, +50% Wick regen; enemies inside burn 40%/s | *Walking:* follows you. *Warding:* stops projectiles. *Pyre:* ends with 600% blast |\n347\t| **Long Night's End** (20) | 50 s | `CastBig`, `Triumph` | 7 s: 3 comets a second, 220% in 2.5 m | *Meteor:* one comet, 1800%. *Drizzle:* 5 a second, weaker. *Dawn:* party heals 5%/s |\n348\t\n349\t**Vows:** *Long Wick* (+20 Wick). *Bright Eyes* (+10% crit against burning). *Keeper of Lamps* (+1 Lamp Post). *Last Light* (survive a fatal hit, once per 90 s).\n350\t\n351\t#### The Sexton: summoner and curse\n352\t\n353\tTends the graves and rings the passing bell. Stovepipe hat, black frock, spade. Raises what they bury, at union rates. Medium-high life, speed 5.2.\n354\t\n355\t**Tithe** (0 to 100) comes from generators and +3 for each nearby death.\n356\t**Graveyard Shift:** corpses remain as Graves for 12 s, and some skills consume them.\n357\t\n358\t| Skill (level) | Cost / cooldown | Clip | Effect | Tones |\n359\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n360\t| **Shovel Swat** (1) | Generator +8 | `ShovelSwat` *(new)* | 140%, arc, 2.6 m | *Spade Edge:* stun 0.3 s. *Flat Side:* slower, 180%, knockback. *Long Handle:* 3.6 m |\n361\t| **Bone Toss** (2) | Generator +5 | `Cast` upper | 110% lob, 14 m, 1.2 m burst | *Ribcage:* 3 shards. *Marrow:* poison 3 s. *Skull:* homing |\n362\t| **Raise Pallbearers** (4) | 30 Tithe, 3 s | `RaiseHands` *(new)* | Up to 4 skeleton minions, 60% each, until killed | *Coffin Crew:* 2 big ones that slam. *Quick Step:* faster. *Volatile:* explode on death |\n363\t| **Exhume** (6) | 20 Tithe | `CastBig` | Explodes up to 5 Graves within 12 m: 300% each, 3 m | *Chain:* blasts make Graves burst. *Feast:* heal 2% each. *Rise:* each leaves a 6 s skeleton |\n364\t| **Coffin Drop** (9) | 9 s | `CastWeapon` | Coffin crashes at target: 380%, 3 m, stun 1 s, stays 4 s as an obstacle | *Family Plot:* 3 coffins. *Occupied:* releases a skeleton. *Lead-Lined:* 700%, 16 s |\n365\t| **Last Rites** (12) | 30 Tithe | `Cast` | 8 s curse, 6 m: enemies take +25%; deaths inside give +6 Tithe | *Slow March:* 30% slow. *Wasting:* 60%/s. *Following:* follows the target |\n366\t| **Gravewalk** (16) | 7 s | `Roll` *(new sink)* | Sink and rise 9 m away: 150% burst, invulnerable 0.4 s | *Deep:* 2 charges. *Hands:* root 1.5 s at exit. *Whole Family:* minions come along |\n367\t| **Danse Macabre** (20) | 60 s | `Triumph` | 10 s: minions +50%, every Grave within 14 m rises, up to 12 extra skeletons | *Tarantella:* +speed. *Finale:* all explode at the end. *Encore:* 40 s, lasts 6 s |\n368\t\n369\t**Vows:** *Union Rates* (+1 minion). *Plot Holder* (Graves last 20 s, +10% Grave damage). *Perpetual Care* (minions heal you 1% per hit). *Mourner's Due* (+2 Tithe per death).\n370\t\n371\t### Effect ops\n372\t\n373\tSkills are data, not code. `src/sim/effects.js` implements these ops and nothing else; skills and Tones only compose them:\n374\t\n375\t`arc, circle, cone, ring, projectile, lob, chain, dash, leap, blink, zone, obstacle, summon, turret, buff, debuff, pull, knock, stun, apply_status, consume_status, consume_graves, comet_rain, delay, repeat, spawn_decoy`\n376\t\n377\t```js\n378\tgreat_toll: { name: 'Great Toll', cost: 30, tags: ['toll', 'sound'], clip: 'BellOverhead', wind: 0.22, rec: 0.3,\n379\t  fx: [{ op: 'ring', radius: 6, dmg: 3.0, element: 'sound', knock: 3, consume: 'resonance', vfx: 'ring_bronze', sfx: 'bell_big', shake: 0.4 }],\n380\t  tones: { twin_peal: { name: 'Twin Peal', add: [{ op: 'delay', t: 0.5, then: { op: 'ring', radius: 6, dmg: 2.1 } }] } } }\n381\t```\n382\t\n383\t---\n384\t\n385\t## 5. Loot and progression\n386\t\n387\t### Formulas\n388\t\n389\t```text\n390\thit         = weaponDps × skill% × (1 + Power/100) × element bonus × (crit? 1.5 + critDmg) × tuning.playerDamage\n391\treduction   = armour / (armour + 50 × monsterLevel)\n392\tmonster HP  = 20 × 1.15^(L-1) × type × (1 + 0.6 × (players-1)) × difficulty × tuning.monsterHp\n393\tmonster hit = 6 × 1.12^(L-1) × type × difficulty × tuning.monsterDamage\n394\thero life   = 40 + 10 × level + 8 × Vitality\n395\tweaponDps   = 8 × 1.13^itemLevel × rarity × random(0.9, 1.1)\n396\txpToNext(L) = round(60 × L^1.75 + 40)\n397\tmonster XP  = (6 + 3 × L) × type\n398\t```\n399\t\n400\tStarting stats: Power 10, Vitality 10, plus 3 Power and 2 Vitality per level. Level cap 30, then Devotion. Monster level is the highest party level, floored by zone: Causeway 1, Ossuary 6, Cloister 11, Belfry 15. Numbers over 9,999 print as `12.4K`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240","uuid":"553cede4-50db-4df9-ab1c-5f6e06b05c56","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:20:48.001Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162042__x14746a/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Game plan: a gothic action RPG for the TV, inspired by Diablo 3\n\n**Genre:** co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash loot game). **Camera:** fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic.\n**Players:** 1 to 4, on one screen or in AI Hub sessions. **Engine:** the Pixel Engine kit (0.46.0 when this was written).\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- TASK.md is the CEO's brief and wins over this plan. This plan decides everything TASK.md leaves open, so you can build without asking.\n- Read in this order: this plan, then the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md` whole, then the hub's `README.md` whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- **Build order is in section 15.** Follow it. The deliverable HTML must build and run from the first milestone on, and every milestone leaves the game playable.\n- Lines marked **(verified)** were run on this computer against engine 0.46.0 in a throwaway build. Trust them. If your kit is newer, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first.\n- Numbers are starting values. Anything about balance lives in one table (`src/data/tuning.js`) and shows up as a dev-mode slider.\n- No more source research is needed. Section 1 says what to take from the source.\n- All paths are relative to your dev folder. Your TASK.md gives the names and places of the dev folder, the deliverable and the exit interview file.\n- **Other agents share this computer.** TASK.md's \"This computer\" notes apply as written, with one correction (section 2, item 5). Stay out of the folders beside yours. Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp`, which is shared. Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`; the browsers are already downloaded. Keep one or two browsers open at most. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n- **No stubs.** If you run out of budget, cut whole features in the order given in section 15, never halfway. A feature that isn't there is fine; a menu item that does nothing is not.\n\n### What you hand back\n\n1. **One HTML file,** named by the pattern in your TASK.md and placed where it says, at the root above the dev folder. It holds the whole game: the engine, your code and the hub client, inlined. TASK.md allows CDN dependencies, but this game needs none.\n2. **The exit interview file** beside that HTML, in the exact shape in section 17.\n3. **Your final chat reply,** carrying Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback. The same four appear on the in-game About/Credits screen (section 13).\n\n**The bar:** a feature-complete demo, treated as a complete, polished vertical slice, long enough to show every feature and mechanic so a player understands how the game plays and feels and what they can and can't do. It carries all the infrastructure the rest of the game would need. No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features.\n\n### The title\n\n**Pick it first:** the names of your dev folder and your HTML include it. As TASK.md says: a playful riff on the source's name; silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator by running code. Make it a pun that reads as its own name, not the source's name with a letter changed. It lives in one constant, `TITLE` in `src/data/meta.js`, and appears only in the game's frame: title screen, attract overlay, session card, About, the no-WebGPU page, `<title>`, the engine's game `name` and the save key. Story text never mentions it, so the world below works with any title.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n### Pitch\n\nForty days ago the great bell that kept the dead asleep cracked. It still tolls every dusk, but now it says *get up*, and everything buried does. You got up too, except that you kept your wits. Pick a trade (swing a holy bell on a chain, hurl lanterns, or bury your problems with a shovel), smash hordes that burst apart, chase loot that rings like church bells, and climb the broken tower to silence the walled-up saint who has been screaming at you the whole way. Alone, on a couch, or online.\n\n### Pillars (when in doubt, these decide)\n\n1. **Every button press breaks something.** Hits have weight: flash, shake, knockback, ragdolls, shattered props. Hordes are the point.\n2. **Loot you can hear.** Rarity is a colour, a beam and a sound. Legendary drops ring a bell. Good items change how skills work, not only the numbers.\n3. **Builds are play styles.** Six slots, eight skills per class, three variations of each. Swapping is free and instant, so experimenting costs nothing.\n4. **Gothic melodrama with a straight face and a black joke.** Villains proclaim. Townsfolk shrug. Nobody winks at the camera.\n5. **A TV game.** Big, readable, one screen at a time, playable from a sofa with a pad. Mouse and keyboard feel native on the same screens.\n6. **One more run.** After the story, a repeatable run mode with a meter, a boss and rising difficulty.\n\n### What we take from the source, and what we make ours\n\nTASK.md asks you to capture what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, and turn it into mechanics, pacing and UX. So the first rows are the feel of the thing, made ours.\n\n| What the source does well | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| A gothic land where hell climbs up through its graveyards, and a doomed town holding out | **Vespermoor** and **Tollhaven** |\n| A hero who is more than mortal | **The Late**: dead, up again, and still themselves |\n| A grim old keeper of lore who reads you the history | **Crier Maudlin**, a severed head who never stopped working |\n| Themes of apocalypse, failing faith and old sins coming due | A cracked holy bell, a wronged saint, a bishop in debt |\n| Hordes that explode, physical destruction | Ragdolls, shatter props, a kill-streak bonus called **Requiem** |\n| Classes with their own resource | **Bellwarden** (Clangor), **Lamplighter** (Wick), **Sexton** (Tithe) |\n| Skills with swappable modifiers | 8 skills per class, each with 3 **Tones** |\n| Passive choices | 4 **Vows** per class, pick 2 |\n| Life pickups that drop in combat | **Tallow**: wax drops that re-melt your life candle |\n| Beams and sounds for great loot | Coloured beams, and a bell strike for every legendary |\n| Elites with random powers | Elite packs with **Afflictions** |\n| The loot creature you chase | **The Collection Plate**: a living offering dish on little legs, spilling coins as it runs |\n| Risky chests | **Unquiet Coffins**: open one, survive 45 seconds, get paid |\n| Temporary shrines | **Votive Shrines** |\n| Repeatable random dungeon with a meter and a boss | **Knell Runs**; the timed version is the **Grand Knell** |\n| Endless levelling | **Devotion** |\n| Difficulty ladder | **Pilgrim, Zealot, Martyr, Penance I to V** |\n| Crafting artisans | **Dame Brasse** the bellfounder, **Mother Ogive** at the Reliquary |\n| Extracting item powers | The **Reliquary** holds three extracted powers |\n| Waypoints, town portal | **Lychgates**, the **Passing Bell** |\n| An AI companion | **Sister Pyx** |\n| Couch and online co-op | Couch co-op on its own, shared-world sessions in AI Hub |\n| Scheming lords of the pit who taunt you while you play | **The Bursar** and **Malison**, in subtitled gibberish |\n\n### Stay original\n\nThis is a new game in the same genre, with its own world, cast, art, music and levels. TASK.md says so and it's a hard rule.\n\n- Never use the source's names or look: Sanctuary, Tristram, Caldeum, Westmarch, Horadrim, Nephalem, Cain, Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Imperius, Malthael, Azmodan, Belial, Kanai, Paragon, Torment, its class names and resource names, its famous lines, its melodies, or a HUD built around two orbs. No fallen star, no amnesiac angel.\n- The source is credited by name only on the About screen and in the exit interview.\n- `tools/test.mjs` greps `src/` for these names as whole words (section 16).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Facts verified on this computer\n\nThese save you hours. All were run in a real build here.\n\n**Bundling**\n1. **(verified)** One HTML file boots on `file://` with WebGPU. Bundle with `esbuild game.js --bundle --format=esm --target=es2022 --charset=utf8 --legal-comments=none`, put the result in one inline `<script type=\"module\">`. The engine has no `import.meta` and needs no fetch, so its built-in models and Rapier's wasm work from the file. The result is about 5.3 MB plus your code, with nothing external. Don't minify your own code: the agent that imports the game adds its usage figures to the About screen later, so keep strings findable.\n2. Escape `</script` as `<\\/script` in the bundle. If you insert with `String.replace`, use a function replacer, or `$&` in the code corrupts the file.\n3. **(verified)** Page order that the hub accepts: `<meta charset>`, then `session.js` byte for byte as the first script, then the `hub:*` metas, then `<title>`. Body: `<div id=\"app\"></div>` and the module script.\n4. **(verified)** If you ever need a pack, set `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__['packs/x.json'] = 'data:application/json;base64,...'` in a classic script before the module, then `loadPack('packs/x.json')` works on `file://`. The built-in library is enough for this game, so prefer not to.\n\n**Browsers in tests**\n5. **(verified) Use the kit's flags:** `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` (the list in `check.mjs`). The shorter list in the \"This computer\" notes lost the GPU device within seconds on this engine (`A valid external Instance reference no longer exists`). That error is the flags, not your game.\n6. **(verified)** Software rendering is slow: about 20 fps for a small scene at 1280×720, 3 to 8 fps at 1080p with 40 rigged monsters. So tests advance time with `engine.step(n)` (120 frames took 6 ms in a small scene) and read pixels with `await engine.renderer.capture()` (about 180 ms). Never wait in real time when you can step.\n7. **(verified)** `Engine.headless` works in Node with `buildModel`, `compileClips`, mixers, HUD calls and audio counts. `hud.width` is 480 and `hud.canvas` is null there. `node check.mjs --headless game.js --keys KeyD*30` plays and replays to the same fingerprint. Replays still match with ragdolls and flying chunks in play. Every module must guard `window`, `document` and `navigator` with `typeof`.\n\n**The hub**\n8. **(verified)** A full engine in the harness's iframes passes: 2 players; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; the same with `scenario=host-leave`; 2 players with `scenario=server`; and a game-owned card. Copies were ready in 2 to 6.5 seconds.\n9. **(verified)** For `AIHub.server` (a copy with no player) use `Engine.headless(game, {})` inside the iframe and drive it with a timer: ready in 0.6 s, no GPU, harness passes. Step by elapsed time, not a fixed count.\n10. **(verified)** On the game's own card, answer Enter from a plain `window` `keydown` listener, not `wasPressed`. The harness allows 3 seconds and software-rendered frames are slow.\n11. The hub's clocks (from `round.js`): hard limit is `hub:session-minutes` × 1.5, at most 15 minutes; 5 s grace after `time-up`; 3 s countdown; 20 s load grace; 20 s hidden-host limit.\n\n**Characters and animation**\n12. **(verified)** You can build any humanoid as a `ModelDef` of nested pivots named after `HERO_RIG`'s joints, and `compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, build.object)` animates it with all 78 hero clips, including `Slash1`, `Slash2`, `Slash3`, `Slam`, `LeapSlam`, `Spin`, `Cast`, `CastBig`, `CastWeapon`, `CastBigWeapon`, `BowDraw`, `BowRelease`, `Roll`, `Charge`, `Shout`, `HitReact`, `Death`, `Triumph`. `new Ragdoll(physics, root, HERO_RAGDOLL)` works on the same body. Template in section 8.\n13. **(verified)** All 15 joints must exist or `compileClips` throws `rig joint \"ForearmR\" not found`. An empty pivot is fine, which keeps cheap monsters cheap (14 meshes against 21).\n14. **(verified)** Compile clips once per body type (63 ms for 78 clips) and share them: `new THREE.AnimationMixer(instance).clipAction(clip)` moves a second instance. `buildModel` is cheap and shares geometry.\n15. **(verified)** Custom clips: `{ name, frames, fast, keys: [[frame, { ArmR: [x, y, z], Pelvis: { p: [x, y, z] } }, 'outCubic'], ...] }` and `gaitClip(HERO_RIG, {...})` compile with `validateClip` clean.\n16. **(verified)** `analyzeClip` throws on merged pivot bodies because its sole meshes are gone. Run it on the engine's own hero inside `Engine.headless`: `const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL)`, same rig, same results. It finds real problems such as `planted foot slides 0.80 m/s`; tune `speed` and `stance` until it's clean.\n17. **(verified)** To attack while running, filter a compiled clip to upper-body tracks: `new THREE.AnimationClip(name, clip.duration, clip.tracks.filter(t => UPPER.has(t.name.split('.')[0])))` and play it over `Run`. Track names look like `Torso.quaternion`. It ran without error; check how it looks.\n\n**Pause, input, camera, HUD**\n18. **(verified) Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`.** `update` keeps running with `dt` 0, `fixedUpdate` stops, `wasPressed` still works, so menus work. `engine.paused = true` stops `update` too: never use it for menus. Menu animation counts rendered frames, since `dt` is 0.\n19. `hitstop` and `timeScale` stall the whole simulation. Never use them on a session host. Use them solo and on the couch only.\n20. **(verified)** Use `aspect: 'fill'`. The default letterboxes (at 1280×720 you get 960×540 in the middle). With `fill` the art size changes with the window: 427×240 at 720p, 480×270 at 1080p and 4K, 512×288 at 1440p, about 136×276 on a portrait phone. Lay out from `hud.width` and `hud.height`, never from 480×270.\n21. **(verified)** The `iso` camera with yaw 45 looks toward −X −Z: screen right is (+0.71, 0, −0.71), screen up is (−0.71, 0, −0.71). So a wall on a room's −X or −Z side shows its face to the camera, and a wall on the +X or +Z side blocks the view.\n22. **(verified)** `camera: { preset: 'iso', viewHeight: 11.5, pitch: 42, yaw: 45 }` looks right at 1080p: a hero about 25 art pixels tall, with 40 monsters on screen and still readable. A `viewHeight` of 13 still reads, which gives the room to zoom out for co-op.\n23. **(verified)** The rig zooms on the wheel after `update` runs. Read `ctx.input.wheel` yourself, then set `ctx.input.wheel = 0` to stop it. `setZoom` clamps to `minZoom` and `maxZoom`.\n24. **(verified)** `hud.text` uppercases for you and draws `?` for any character it lacks. It has digits, A to Z, straight and curly quotes, the backtick and `. , : ; - + = / ( ) [ ] { } % _ ! ? < > # * @ ° · & | ~ ^ $`. No em dash, no ×, no arrows. Text is 6 art pixels per character (minus one) and 7 tall. `hudSprite` icons are about 12×12. A test lints all strings against this set.\n25. **(verified)** The engine's `Input` merges every pad into one, which can't do couch co-op. Set `ctx.input.gamepadButtons = {}` and read `navigator.getGamepads()` yourself, per pad (guard with `typeof`). A stub via `addInitScript` reaches both. The kit's pad mapping is for the platformer and would press Space, KeyJ and so on.\n26. Mouse buttons are keys (`MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`), and the canvas already blocks the context menu. The HUD canvas has `pointer-events: none`, so the pointer reaches the game through it.\n\n**Audio**\n27. **(verified)** Engine defaults are master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, saved under `pixel-engine:audio`, a key shared by every engine game on the same origin. So on first run (your own flag in storage) call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. `audio.resetVolumes()`, which the engine's settings panel calls, puts it back to 0.6, so wrap that method on the instance to re-apply 0.1. That doesn't touch `pixel-engine.js`.\n\n**Touch and look**\n28. **(verified)** Detect touch-only with `!matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` (true on Chromium's Pixel 7 profile, false on desktop). `touch: false` removes the joystick. The ⚙ button stays visible. WebKit here has no WebGPU, so test touch in Chromium.\n29. **(verified) The mood:** `engine.sun.color.setHex(PALETTE.sky); engine.sun.intensity = 0.55; engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy); engine.ambient.intensity = 0.9; scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.ink);` plus library torches and a warm light following the hero gives a rich dark dungeon. A tall thin `glowMaterial` box plus a coloured point light on the floor makes a superb loot beam.\n30. **(verified)** Floor textures at full white are too loud against monsters. `toonMaterial(PALETTE.mist, { map })` pushes them back and monsters read much better; `flagstones` tinted that way looked best, and `ashlar` is busy, so keep it for walls. Bright red `Decals` at 1 to 2 m look cartoonish; `plum` at 0.4 to 0.9 m sits right.\n\n**Randomness**\n31. **(verified) `Rng.fork` depends on the parent's current state,** whatever the docs' comment suggests: once the parent has drawn a number, the same `fork('zone:1')` gives different numbers. So keep one root `new Rng(seed)` per world that only forks and never draws. Forks from that root are stable, whatever their children draw. Every session client relies on this to build the same map.\n\n**Also run under `Engine.headless` and working (verified):** `Breakables` (`add`, then `break`); `Timeline` (cues, `line()`, `bars()`, `skip()`); `NavGrid` (`flowTo`, `flowDirection`, `path`, `lineOfSight`, `setOpen`); `LegStepper` with `twoBoneIK`; `SpringChain`; `Squash`; `Decals`; `Trail`; `Precipitation`; `WaterSurface`; `groundFog`; `{ dissolve: true }` clones; `glowMaterial` swaps; `mergeStaticMeshes` with a tinted `textureMap`; `disposeObject` on a zone root; `camera.rayAt` onto the ground; `lights.request` with `lifetime` and `priority`; `screen.cover`, `reveal`, `shockwave` and `flash`; `input.send` reaching `input.commands`; `audio.loop` with `spatialize`; the bell as sine `layers` (peak 0.84 from `renderSound`); `parseSong` on the Clemency motif. These ran without error; how they look is yours to check.\n\n**This engine start worked (verified):**\n\n```js\nconst engine = await Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({\n  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n  camera: { preset: 'iso', viewHeight: 11.5, pitch: 42, yaw: 45, minZoom: 0.6, maxZoom: 1.4 },\n  aspect: 'fill', quality: 'high', touch: false,\n}));\n```\n\nAdd `settings: { button: touchOnly ? 'top-right' : false }` to that **(verified: `false` removes the button)**. Your own Settings screen replaces the ⚙ everywhere except touch-only devices, where TASK.md asks for it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. World, story and tone\n\n### The world\n\n**Vespermoor** is a drowned moorland kingdom under one enormous tower, the **Belfry of Saint Malison**. In it hangs the Great Bell, **Old Clemency**. For a thousand years it tolled at dusk, and the toll told everything below: *stay down*. Forty days ago it cracked. It still tolls. It now says something else.\n\n- **The Risen:** the mindless dead, up and about.\n- **The Unrung:** demons from Below, things no bell was ever rung for. They hate sound, and they've come up through the graves the dead left open.\n- **The Late (formally the Tollborn):** you. You died during the cracked toll, or just after, and got up with your mind intact. Townsfolk find this awkward. They call you *the Late* and ask you to wipe your feet. Death doesn't stick to you, which is why you respawn.\n- **Tollhaven:** the last parish with its lamps lit.\n\n**The truth:** Saint Malison, revered as the foundress who \"gave herself to the bell\", was walled up alive in the tower's foundation by the first bishops, singing. She has heard that bell every dusk for a thousand years. Bishop Ambrose, drowning in debts to a demon called the Bursar, cracked it for her. She wants one thing: to ring the Last Toll, **Eventide**, and wake everything ever buried. The only thing she still fears is the sound of Old Clemency rung true.\n\n### Cast\n\n| Who | Role | Voice and manner |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Crier Maudlin** | Quest giver, reads lore aloud. A severed head in a lantern on a post. Lost the body, kept the job. | High, reedy, unstoppable |\n| **Dame Brasse** | Bellfounder: salvage, craft, reforge. Melts church bells into swords. Half deaf. | LOUD. Everything shouted |\n| **Mother Ogive** | Keeper of the Reliquary: extract powers, sockets. Blind; knows relics by taste. | Soft, delighted, unsettling |\n| **Pennywhistle** | Merchant in a plague-doctor beak. | Cheerful, nasal, modest markups considering the apocalypse |\n| **Sister Pyx** | Companion. Armoured nun with a crossbow. | Deadpan |\n| **Bishop Ambrose Thrice-Buried** | Kindly, pale, keeps a spade in the coffin. The traitor. | Warm, then not |\n| **The Bursar** | Mid-game boss. A bloated demon accountant in a stolen cope. | Booming, delighted by interest |\n| **Malison, the Unrung Saint** | Final boss. Huge entombed saint wrapped in bell ropes. | Vast, layered, furious |\n| **The Narrator** | Opening and ending. | Deep and slow |\n\n### Tone\n\nThree registers, and write everything in one of them:\n\n1. **High gothic proclamation:** narration, lore books, Malison. Long vowels, doom, an ominous last line.\n2. **Gallows humour:** townsfolk, item flavour, death messages. Dry, never modern. No slang, no winks.\n3. **Boss taunts:** direct address, mid-fight, short.\n\nSubtitles fit two rows of about 60 characters, which every line below does. Lore books get a paged screen instead. Punctuation stays inside the HUD font (section 2, item 24). Use these as written and write the rest to match.\n\n**Narrator:** \"For a thousand years Old Clemency tolled at dusk, and the dead of Vespermoor stayed down.\" / \"Forty days ago the Great Bell cracked. It still tolls. It now says something else.\" / \"You were buried on the thirty-ninth day. It did not take.\"\n\n**Crier Maudlin:** \"Hear ye, hear ye! The dead are up, the roads are closed, and the fish is off. Also you. You are dead. Welcome back.\" / \"I lost the body at harvest and kept the job. No one told me to stop.\" / \"The parish asks that you wipe your feet. You have been in the ground.\"\n\n**Dame Brasse:** \"WHAT? SPEAK UP! I MELT BELLS FOR A LIVING!\" / \"THAT SWORD WAS A CHURCH BELL ON TUESDAY. HIT SOMETHING WITH IT AND IT STILL SAYS AMEN.\"\n\n**Mother Ogive:** \"Bring me the holy bones, child. I will know them by the taste.\" / \"A saint's knuckle. Very old. A little salty. She died angry.\"\n\n**Pennywhistle:** \"Everything is half price, dear friend. The other half is your soul, but we can discuss terms.\" / \"No refunds. No returns. No survivors, so far.\"\n\n**Sister Pyx:** \"I have killed four hundred things today and confessed to none of them.\" / \"Bless you. Duck.\" / \"You smell like a grave. It is an improvement on the last one.\"\n\n**Bishop Ambrose:** \"They have buried me three times, my child. One learns to keep a spade in the coffin.\"\n\n**The Bursar:** \"YOUR SOUL IS OVERDUE!\" / \"INTEREST COMPOUNDS, LITTLE CORPSE!\" / \"I HAVE YOU DOWN FOR ONE DEATH. I SEE IT WAS NOT COLLECTED.\" / \"FORECLOSURE!\"\n\n**Malison:** \"A THOUSAND YEARS I HAVE BEEN YOUR FOUNDATION STONE. NOW I SHALL BE YOUR CEILING.\" / \"THEY WALLED ME UP SINGING. DO YOU KNOW WHAT I LEARNED IN THE DARK? EVERY VERSE.\" / \"RUN, LITTLE RINGER. THE HOUR IS UNRUNG, AND IT IS YOURS.\" / \"LISTEN. THAT IS EVERY GRAVE YOU EVER WALKED ON, OPENING.\" / (on a hero's death) \"DOWN YOU GO. GIVE MY REGARDS TO THE WORMS.\"\n\n**Lore book:** \"THE THIRD NIGHT. THE GRAVES IN THE NEW YARD ARE EMPTY. THE GRAVES IN THE OLD YARD ARE EMPTY. I HAVE BEGUN DIGGING ONE FOR MYSELF, FOR THE COMPANY.\"\n\n**System lines:** Death: \"YOU HAVE DIED. AGAIN. IT IS BECOMING A HABIT.\" Level up: \"YOU FEEL MORE ALIVE. THIS IS NOT MEDICALLY POSSIBLE.\" Streak: \"37 KILL REQUIEM\".\n\n**Item flavour:** \"She rang it only once. It was enough.\" / \"The best one is still in him.\" / \"Smells of smoke and regret.\"\n\n### The story, in five quests\n\n| # | Quest | Where | What happens | Unlocks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Wipe Your Feet** | St. Clement's Yard | Your coffin bursts. Tutorial fights. The Crier's voice carries over the wall. Reach Tollhaven. | Town, Pennywhistle, potion |\n| 2 | **The Weeping Causeway** | The Causeway | Find Dame Brasse in a gibbet camp. Defend the wagon of bells on the way back. Sister Pyx joins. | Bellfounder, Lychgates, Pyx |\n| 3 | **Saint Gloam's Bones** | Ossuary, 3 floors | Mother Ogive needs three relic bones. Under the third floor is the Counting House and **the Bursar**, who has a ledger with the Bishop's name in it. | Reliquary, sockets, first legendary guaranteed |\n| 4 | **The Cinder Cloister** | Burning abbey | Rescue the last novices. Malison's voice taunts you openly now. Confront **Bishop Ambrose**, who is buried and bursts out three times. The Crier reads the truth about the saint. | Passing the Belfry gate |\n| 5 | **Ring It or Die Trying** | The Cracked Belfry | Climb the tower. Fight **Malison** beneath the bell. Ring Old Clemency by hand. | Ending, credits, Penance, Knell Runs |\n\n**Ending:** the bell rings true, Malison comes apart, dawn hits the moor. Over the credits, far below, a much deeper bell answers. It's the hook for the rest of the game.\n\n**Side content:** the **Drowned Pantry** (optional small cellar off the Causeway, mini-boss the **Cellarer**, a \"return the overdue book\" joke quest for the Crier); 12 lore books (read aloud, kept in the Codex); **Writs** (section 7).\n\n### The first five minutes\n\n| Time | Beat |\n|---|---|\n| 0:00 | Title, New Hero, pick a class |\n| 0:10 | Narrator over black, a cracked bell toll; 20 seconds, skippable |\n| 0:30 | Night, rain, graveyard. A coffin bursts; hero plays `GetUp`. Prompt: move |\n| 0:40 | Three Peatwalkers. Prompt: attack. They burst. Urns and a barrel shatter, gold rains |\n| 1:30 | Tallow drops, the candle re-melts. A short-beam blue weapon drops. Toast: \"UPGRADE: [glyph] EQUIP\" |\n| 2:30 | Level 2, second skill unlocked, prompt to assign |\n| 3:00 | First telegraph: a red ring grows. Prompt: dodge |\n| 4:00 | An Unquiet Coffin at the gate. 45 seconds of chaos, a chest of gold, level 3 |\n| 5:00 | Tollhaven. \"Hear ye, hear ye!\" |\n\nPacing target: about 60 minutes to the credits for a first player. Tutorial 5, town 3, Causeway 12, Ossuary 15, town 2, Cloister 12, Belfry 8 with Malison, ending 2. A Knell Run takes 6 to 8 minutes.\n\n**New Hero** offers two starts: **The Beginning**, or **The Last Bell**: a level 30 hero with every skill and Tone unlocked, three legendaries, two pieces of its class set, materials and gems, 5 Devotion points to spend and a Grand Key, with the town open and Knell Runs available. Devotion needs level 30 and the story ends near level 20, so this start is what lets anyone see the whole endgame at once.\n\n### Where the player meets each feature\n\nTASK.md wants every feature shown, so none of them is left to luck. The playthrough tests in section 16 assert every row.\n\n| Feature | First met |\n|---|---|\n| Move, attack, dodge, potion, Tallow, gold, smashing props, telegraphs | St. Clement's Yard (tutorial) |\n| Item drops, rarity beams, one-button equip | Yard: the first upgrade is scripted |\n| Votive Shrine, Unquiet Coffin | Yard: both scripted |\n| Skill slots, Tones | Level 2 and level 3, with prompts |\n| Vows | Level 5 (first) and level 15 (second) |\n| Pennywhistle, Collection Box, Crier, Codex, How to Play, difficulty change | Tollhaven, after quest 1 |\n| Dame Brasse (salvage, craft, reforge), Lychgates, Passing Bell, Sister Pyx, Writs | Quest 2 |\n| Champion pack, the Collection Plate, an escort event, the side dungeon | Causeway: the first Plate and first pack are scripted |\n| Rare pack, traps and levers, gems and sockets, Mother Ogive (extract, Reliquary), first boss, first legendary | Ossuary |\n| The Unrung (demons), a defence event, second boss | Cloister |\n| Set items, final boss, ending | Belfry |\n| Penance tiers, Knell Runs | After the credits, or at once with The Last Bell |\n| Grand Knell, Devotion, set bonus | The Last Bell at once, or by playing on |\n| Couch co-op | Any time: a toast appears when a second pad is seen, and Party is in the pause menu |\n| AI Hub round | When the hub launches the game |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Combat\n\n### Rules\n\n- **Simulation:** 60 Hz in `fixedUpdate`, on a flat world (y = 0). Hero speed 5 to 5.6 m/s, radius 0.4 m. Bodies push softly; a hero stuck for 0.3 s passes through small monsters.\n- **Cast states:** wind-up, hit frame, recovery. Attack speed scales wind-up and recovery. Move input cancels the second half of recovery; dodge cancels anything. Hold a button to repeat.\n- **Moving while casting:** the Lamplighter's Spark Bolt and Sexton's Bone Toss play upper-body clips over `Run` (section 2, item 17). Everything else roots the hero for its wind-up.\n- **Dodge:** a 0.35 s `Roll`, 4.5 m, invulnerable throughout, 3 s cooldown (slider).\n- **Potion:** infinite, heals 60%, 30 s cooldown.\n- **Tallow:** kills drop it at 8% (elites always). Walking over it heals 20%.\n- **Gold** flies to you inside the pick-up radius (3 m). **Items** are picked up by walking over them (inventory has 40 slots; when full, they stay on the ground with a message).\n- **Telegraphs:** any attack worth more than 25% of a same-level hero's life shows a growing red ground ring or line for at least 0.6 s, with a warning sound. Dodging through it always works.\n- **Requiem:** kills within 1 second of each other chain. At 10+ a banner shows the count and pays bonus XP (count × 2% of the kill XP total).\n- **Death:** the candle goes out. Solo: 0.8 s of slow motion. Options: rise here (5 s wait, plus 5 s for each recent death, up to 30), last Lychgate, or town. No penalty beyond the walk. In co-op an ally revives you by holding interact for 2 s.\n\n### Feel recipe (apply to every hit)\n\nUse all of these and tune with the \"feel\" slider.\n\n- Target flashes: swap `mesh.material` to a shared `glowMaterial(PALETTE.white)` for 2 frames. Don't clone materials per monster.\n- Knockback slide; `ctx.particles.burst` tinted by element; a pitched-up hit sound; a damage number that pops and drifts. Crits use scale 2 and `sand`.\n- Big hits: `engine.shake.add(0.1 to 0.5)`, `engine.screen.shockwave(at, { radius })` on novas, `hitstop(0.04 to 0.08)` when solo or on the couch only.\n- **Kills:** humanoids go into `Ragdoll` with velocity away from the hit, scaled by overkill. Keep up to 16 live ragdolls; older ones freeze and dissolve (`dissolve: true` on a per-body clone at death only). Others squash, burst and dissolve. `Decals`: small `plum` splats and `scorch`.\n- **Props:** urns, barrels, crates, coffins, tombstones have HP. On break, hide the library model and do `breaks.break(breaks.add({ at, size, color, cuts }), hitAt, speed)` so chunks fly. They drop gold 40% and Tallow 5%.\n- **Elements:** Sound (`sand`, `white`, expanding rings), Flame (`orange`, `red`, `sand`, a warm point light), Grave (`lime`, `plum`, smoke, a green point light). Lights use `lifetime` and priority 3; heroes 4, loot 2, torches 1.\n\n### Targeting\n\n- **Pad:** skills target softly. The best monster inside a 120° cone of facing and the skill's range gets a reticle ring. Right stick overrides aim and moves the ground reticle for ground-target skills. Melee gets a 0.6 m lunge assist.\n- **Mouse:** hero faces the pointer. Left click on a monster walks into range and attacks; on an NPC or object, walks and uses it; on the ground, moves (hold to keep steering). Shift-click attacks in place. `camera.rayAt` gives the ground point (solve for y = 0); pick monsters by screen distance to their chest (14 art pixels), items by their label rectangle.\n- **Keyboard:** WASD or arrows also move.\n\n### Classes\n\nEveryone has 6 slots. Slots open at levels 1, 1, 3, 6, 9, 12. Any skill can go in any slot. Swap freely out of combat (no cost, 1 s lockout on the changed slot). Each skill has an unlock level; its three Tones unlock at +2, +6 and +11 levels after it (capped at 30). The first Vow opens at level 5, the second at level 15.\n\nPercentages are of weapon damage. \"Generator\" means it gives resource and costs nothing. Clips are engine clips unless marked *(new)*.\n\n#### The Bellwarden: melee bruiser\n\nA guardian of the bell-ringers' order who swings a consecrated hand-bell the size of an anvil on a chain. High life, armour +30%, speed 5.0.\n\n**Clangor** (0 to 100) builds on hits and drains after 4 s out of combat.\n**Resonance:** every hit adds a stack to the monster (max 5, 6 s). *Toll* skills consume stacks for +25% damage each. Show stacks as small rings on the monster.\n\n| Skill (level) | Cost / cooldown | Clip | Effect | Tones |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Clout** (1) | Generator +10 | `Slash1`, `Slash2`, `Slash3` chain | 150%, 120° arc, 2.8 m | *Ringing:* chains 60% to 2 more. *Heavy:* slower, 230%, stun 0.4 s. *Pious:* heal 2% per target |\n| **Chain Reap** (2) | Generator +6 per target | `Spin` once | 110% circle, 4 m | *Drag:* pulls 1.5 m. *Long Chain:* 5.5 m. *Rust:* bleed 40%/s for 3 s |\n| **Great Toll** (4) | 30 Clangor, Toll | `BellOverhead` *(new)* | 300% ring, 6 m, knockback 3 m | *Twin Peal:* second ring after 0.5 s at 70%. *Cracked:* leaves shock patches 4 s. *Deaf:* 200%, stun 1 s |\n| **Reverberate** (6) | 20 Clangor, Toll | `Shout` | 260% wave, cone, 9 m, passes through | *Echo:* bounces off walls once. *Narrow:* 60% narrower, 420%. *Hymn:* +3 Clangor per target |\n| **Bellfall** (9) | 10 s | `LeapSlam` | Leap up to 12 m; 400%, 2 m, a bell falls with you | *Iron Bell:* leaves a taunting bell 5 s, 100%/s. *Swift:* 6 s, 250%. *Comet:* burning trail |\n| **Clapper Charge** (12) | 8 s | `Charge` | Dash 10 m, 200%, shoves aside | *Battering:* stun at end. *Relentless:* kills refund 50% cooldown. *Wake:* sound trail 80%/s |\n| **Hallowed Ground** (16) | 20 s | `CastBigWeapon` | 8 s ring, 7 m: allies take 30% less, heal 3%/s | *Vigil:* immune to stuns. *Ire:* +25% damage. *Procession:* follows you |\n| **Last Peal** (20) | 45 s | `Spin` loop | 8 s spin: 180% per 0.25 s, 4.5 m, 20% slower, unstoppable | *Cyclone:* pulls. *Thunder:* ends with 800%. *Eternal:* kills add 0.3 s |\n\n**Vows (pick 2):** *Iron* (+20% armour). *Clamour* (+20 Clangor, no drain for 8 s). *Contrition* (+15% damage under half life). *Silence* (kills give +15% speed for 3 s).\n\n#### The Lamplighter: ranged kiter\n\nLast of the guild that keeps night at bay. Long coat, wide hat, a lamp pole on the back. Medium life, speed 5.6.\n\n**Wick** (0 to 100) regenerates 14/s.\n**Kindle:** Flame hits Burn (30%/s for 4 s). Burning enemies take +15% from your skills.\n\n| Skill (level) | Cost / cooldown | Clip | Effect | Tones |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Spark Bolt** (1) | Generator +4 | `CastWeapon` upper | 120% fast bolt, 18 m, Burns | *Split:* pierce 1, forks on kill. *Swift:* 1.4× speed, 90%. *Star:* arcs to 2 more |\n| **Flare** (2) | 20 Wick | `Cast` | 220% cone, 8 m, monsters miss for 1.5 s | *Phosphor:* 50% wider. *Magnesium:* 340%, no blind. *Signal:* marks, allies +20% for 5 s |\n| **Lantern Hurl** (4) | 30 Wick | `LanternLob` *(new)* | Lob up to 14 m: 240% in 3 m, burning pool 4 s at 60%/s | *Cluster:* 3 small. *Oil:* 8 s pool. *Blue Flame:* 40% slow |\n| **Lamp Post** (6) | 12 s, max 3 | `Dig` *(new)* | Plants a lamp 15 s, fires 70% bolts twice a second | *Arc Lamp:* beams between lamps. *Bright:* heals allies in 4 m. *Bomb:* explodes 500% at the end |\n| **Dusk Step** (9) | 6 s | `Roll` | Blink 8 m, ember decoy 2 s | *Double:* 2 charges. *Scorch:* 180% blast at origin. *Veil:* 2 s unseen, next hit +100% |\n| **Snuff** (12) | 40 Wick | `CastBig` | All Burn within 14 m pays out at once: remaining burn × 3 | *Smother:* stun 1 s. *Relight:* re-Burns after 2 s. *Harvest:* +8 Wick per target |\n| **Beacon** (16) | 25 s | `CastBigWeapon` | 10 s pillar, 7 m: allies +20% damage, +50% Wick regen; enemies inside burn 40%/s | *Walking:* follows you. *Warding:* stops projectiles. *Pyre:* ends with 600% blast |\n| **Long Night's End** (20) | 50 s | `CastBig`, `Triumph` | 7 s: 3 comets a second, 220% in 2.5 m | *Meteor:* one comet, 1800%. *Drizzle:* 5 a second, weaker. *Dawn:* party heals 5%/s |\n\n**Vows:** *Long Wick* (+20 Wick). *Bright Eyes* (+10% crit against burning). *Keeper of Lamps* (+1 Lamp Post). *Last Light* (survive a fatal hit, once per 90 s).\n\n#### The Sexton: summoner and curse\n\nTends the graves and rings the passing bell. Stovepipe hat, black frock, spade. Raises what they bury, at union rates. Medium-high life, speed 5.2.\n\n**Tithe** (0 to 100) comes from generators and +3 for each nearby death.\n**Graveyard Shift:** corpses remain as Graves for 12 s, and some skills consume them.\n\n| Skill (level) | Cost / cooldown | Clip | Effect | Tones |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Shovel Swat** (1) | Generator +8 | `ShovelSwat` *(new)* | 140%, arc, 2.6 m | *Spade Edge:* stun 0.3 s. *Flat Side:* slower, 180%, knockback. *Long Handle:* 3.6 m |\n| **Bone Toss** (2) | Generator +5 | `Cast` upper | 110% lob, 14 m, 1.2 m burst | *Ribcage:* 3 shards. *Marrow:* poison 3 s. *Skull:* homing |\n| **Raise Pallbearers** (4) | 30 Tithe, 3 s | `RaiseHands` *(new)* | Up to 4 skeleton minions, 60% each, until killed | *Coffin Crew:* 2 big ones that slam. *Quick Step:* faster. *Volatile:* explode on death |\n| **Exhume** (6) | 20 Tithe | `CastBig` | Explodes up to 5 Graves within 12 m: 300% each, 3 m | *Chain:* blasts make Graves burst. *Feast:* heal 2% each. *Rise:* each leaves a 6 s skeleton |\n| **Coffin Drop** (9) | 9 s | `CastWeapon` | Coffin crashes at target: 380%, 3 m, stun 1 s, stays 4 s as an obstacle | *Family Plot:* 3 coffins. *Occupied:* releases a skeleton. *Lead-Lined:* 700%, 16 s |\n| **Last Rites** (12) | 30 Tithe | `Cast` | 8 s curse, 6 m: enemies take +25%; deaths inside give +6 Tithe | *Slow March:* 30% slow. *Wasting:* 60%/s. *Following:* follows the target |\n| **Gravewalk** (16) | 7 s | `Roll` *(new sink)* | Sink and rise 9 m away: 150% burst, invulnerable 0.4 s | *Deep:* 2 charges. *Hands:* root 1.5 s at exit. *Whole Family:* minions come along |\n| **Danse Macabre** (20) | 60 s | `Triumph` | 10 s: minions +50%, every Grave within 14 m rises, up to 12 extra skeletons | *Tarantella:* +speed. *Finale:* all explode at the end. *Encore:* 40 s, lasts 6 s |\n\n**Vows:** *Union Rates* (+1 minion). *Plot Holder* (Graves last 20 s, +10% Grave damage). *Perpetual Care* (minions heal you 1% per hit). *Mourner's Due* (+2 Tithe per death).\n\n### Effect ops\n\nSkills are data, not code. `src/sim/effects.js` implements these ops and nothing else; skills and Tones only compose them:\n\n`arc, circle, cone, ring, projectile, lob, chain, dash, leap, blink, zone, obstacle, summon, turret, buff, debuff, pull, knock, stun, apply_status, consume_status, consume_graves, comet_rain, delay, repeat, spawn_decoy`\n\n```js\ngreat_toll: { name: 'Great Toll', cost: 30, tags: ['toll', 'sound'], clip: 'BellOverhead', wind: 0.22, rec: 0.3,\n  fx: [{ op: 'ring', radius: 6, dmg: 3.0, element: 'sound', knock: 3, consume: 'resonance', vfx: 'ring_bronze', sfx: 'bell_big', shake: 0.4 }],\n  tones: { twin_peal: { name: 'Twin Peal', add: [{ op: 'delay', t: 0.5, then: { op: 'ring', radius: 6, dmg: 2.1 } }] } } }\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Loot and progression\n\n### Formulas\n\n```text\nhit         = weaponDps × skill% × (1 + Power/100) × element bonus × (crit? 1.5 + critDmg) × tuning.playerDamage\nreduction   = armour / (armour + 50 × monsterLevel)\nmonster HP  = 20 × 1.15^(L-1) × type × (1 + 0.6 × (players-1)) × difficulty × tuning.monsterHp\nmonster hit = 6 × 1.12^(L-1) × type × difficulty × tuning.monsterDamage\nhero life   = 40 + 10 × level + 8 × Vitality\nweaponDps   = 8 × 1.13^itemLevel × rarity × random(0.9, 1.1)\nxpToNext(L) = round(60 × L^1.75 + 40)\nmonster XP  = (6 + 3 × L) × type\n```\n\nStarting stats: Power 10, Vitality 10, plus 3 Power and 2 Vitality per level. Level cap 30, then Devotion. Monster level is the highest party level, floored by zone: Causeway 1, Ossuary 6, Cloister 11, Belfry 15. Numbers over 9,999 print as `12.4K`.","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1226}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018SwXAxERhZ6JccBFcHzLx4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
