{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgm6LteAGyvmfjsQEiBJG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Toll Booth of Doom** is built, and all four test tiers pass on the final build: 0 failures in 1769 sim, 325 headless, 58 browser and 10 hub checks.\n\nThe deliverable is one 6 MB HTML file, `TollBoothOfDoom-Diablo3-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-162042.html`, in this folder. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it and parses. The dev folder has the source, `PROGRESS.md` and `docs/DESIGNERS.md`. `node tools/test.mjs` runs everything and prints only failures and a count.\n\n## What it is\nA gothic co-op action RPG for the TV, fixed isometric, 1 to 4 players on the Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (the plan's runtime) and the AI Hub protocol.\n- **Heroes:** Bellwarden, Lamplighter and Sexton, with 24 skills and 72 Tones.\n- **World:** five quests, three bosses, and a Tollhaven with town services.\n- **Endgame:** Knell Runs, the Grand Knell, Devotion, Writs and a Last Bell start.\n- **Shell:** pad-first UI that switches PlayStation, Xbox and keyboard prompts, attract mode, dev mode with live sliders, About, touch-only message and a no-WebGPU page.\n- **Audio:** all in code, with music at 10% by default.\n\n## What I cut or reduced\n- **Cut, and removed from the code:** the Drowned Pantry, Sister Pyx, and the hub round's level-up picks. All three are on the plan's cut list.\n- **Reduced:**\n  - **Art:** monsters are simple blocky models, with no Crawler/Clapperjaw leg stepping and no Malison rope arms.\n  - **Rooms:** 10 room templates are shared across all biomes, not 8 per biome.\n  - **Cutscenes:** the opening, ending and boss entrances are narrated overlays and banners, not camera cutscenes.\n- **Pacing not verified:** the bot clears the campaign in 12 to 19 sim minutes, so the plan's 60-minute first-player pace is unchecked.\n- **Not checked at all:** no real pad, TV or listener was available, so those are covered only by the stand-ins the brief allows (stub pads, the Pixel 7 phone profile, Web Audio level readings).\n\n## Testing results\n- **Playthrough:** a bot plays each class from the title through the real menus to the credits, then a full Knell Run.\n  - **Level at credits:** 19.\n  - **Bursar fight:** 62 to 150 s.\n  - **Malison fight:** 123 to 272 s.\n  - **Legendaries dropped:** 6 to 8.\n- **Hub:** all five harness variants pass, plus a two-frame check where one player's hero moves in the other's world.\n- **Screenshots:** I looked at the title, town, gear, skills, couch HUD, attract mode and dev mode. I did not look at every screen.\n\n---\n\n# Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:42\n- **Source:** Diablo 3\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash); fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (with three.js r186 and Rapier inside), JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild 0.28, WebGPU, Web Audio, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native pixel-art toon style and palette, with new models for heroes, NPCs, monsters, bosses and key props; the library for generic props\n- **Animation:** The engine's clip system on its hero rig, 15 new clips including a procedural walking gait, ragdolls on death, and procedural bobbing and squash for the non-humanoid monsters\n- **Audio:** All in code: chiptune songs, synthesised effects, Animalese-style babble for voices, music at 10% by default\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, up to 4; couch co-op on its own\n- **Testing:** Headless scripts that play the game and check its state, a scripted whole-game playthrough, one command, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review\n- **Research:** Read the plan, the kit guide, ASSETS.md and the hub README before building; no other research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n# Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 39 issues were caught by scripts and tests and logged in `tests/ISSUES.md`: unreachable scripted spawns and drops, a quest step that advanced through a locked exit, a loot view crash, boss-arena walls that never lifted, monsters deadlocked at doorways, a class picker that always chose the last card, pause and hero screens that closed the frame they opened, a second pad's Options pausing the first player, 49 layout overflows, an XP compounding bug, boss timers that ran on distance instead of time, and a hub session that never started its simulation.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 19 of them: for example the Bursar's coin crash, missing arena exits, permanent boss walls, the missing relic and rope interactions, lychgates inside walls, doorway deadlocks, the class picker, the pause menu that could not open, the pad pause conflict, scripted spawns inside walls, the tutorial full of monsters, a quest step skipping a locked exit, boss timers flooding the arena with adds, the Knell Run with no monsters in it, and the hub session that never stepped.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The whole-game playthrough by a bot, played through the real menus by key presses, once per class, then a Knell Run. It found most of the softlocks and logic errors no unit test would have asked about. The two-frame hub game test and the Last Bell test were next: they caught the session that never started and the Knell with no monsters, both of which passed the earlier checks.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The text lints (banned names, TODO words, HUD font characters) and screenshots of the title. The lints found only words in comments, and a title screenshot proves very little once the layout test exists.\n\n# Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** One module that holds everything: three.js, Rapier, the hero rig with 78 clips, the toon pixel renderer, audio synthesis and a headless mode. Stepping frames by hand with `engine.step`, recording and replaying with a matching fingerprint, and checking models with the sheets tool made a very large game testable in Node.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. NavGrid centres its grid on the origin unless you pass an origin, which made every path fail until I did. The pixel HUD is 427 by 240 art pixels on a 720p window but 480 by 270 headless, so layouts built for one overflowed in the other. Pads are merged into one input, so couch co-op needed my own pad reader.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The whole simulation and its rules (skills as data ops, loot, monsters, zones from ASCII rooms, bosses, quests), an immediate-mode UI with focus movement for pad, mouse and keyboard, a per-seat input router, the hub netcode, the Animalese voices and the bot that plays the game.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Getting a lot of authored content to read at 480 by 270: pixel text only, no wrapping helpers, and a HUD that is a canvas. Also running anything with many actors, ragdolls and lights under software WebGPU, which is slow enough that everything had to be checked by stepping frames rather than watching.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Expose the audio output so a test can read levels without wrapping `AudioNode.prototype.connect`, read each gamepad separately instead of merging them, and make the headless HUD the same size as a 720p page.\n\n# Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan was detailed and mostly verified on this machine: the build order, the never-cut list, the netcode budgets, the test tiers and the exact flags to start the browser with saved a great deal of guessing.\n- **What was confusing?** How much was expected in one run. Three classes with 24 skills and 72 Tones, three bosses, a hub session with its own card and netcode, four test tiers and a screen for everything is a large game for one sitting. The pacing target of about 60 minutes conflicts with a bot that clears every room, and the art size differs between the plan (480 by 270) and a real 720p window.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Making the playthrough bot reliable. Most of the real bugs surfaced only when a script tried to finish the game by pathing through generated rooms with crowds, doorways and bosses, and every softlock cost a full replay.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Cut the scope to something that fits, or give a reference bot with pathing and stuck handling, and say plainly which art size the HUD layout must target. State the XP target as a number of kills as well as a level, since a clear-everything bot and a human reach very different levels.\n\nThe same four sections are on the in-game About/Credits screen, generated from `exit-interview.json`. Dev mode is under Pause → Dev Mode."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":372,"cache_read_input_tokens":902592,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":372},"output_tokens":840,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7f78278-523d-4db5-bad6-0b641e53d240","uuid":"5d28530c-0fc2-4d14-9106-64e8e6392e3b","timestamp":"2026-11-06T05:06:24.115Z","request_id":"req_011Cgm6LtEMYYwPPsEZ5AMYv"}
